var e=JSON.parse('[[2901,"createListenerMiddleware is middleware built into Redux Toolkit, which lets you run side effects in response to Redux\'s actions. It listens for particular actions and performs callbacks, which is convenient for logging, navigation or calls to an API. Unlike createAsyncThunk, it does not create new actions but merely responds to existing ones.","What is createListenerMiddleware?\\ncreateListenerMiddleware is middleware from Redux Toolkit, which lets you perform arbitrary code (side effects) in response to the dispatching of particular actions. It is similar to sagas or epics, but simpler and lighter to use, since it requires no additional libraries.\\nHow does it work?\\nThe middleware intercepts every dispatch and checks whether it corresponds to the conditions given (to the type of the action, for example). If the condition is met, a callback is run, which can perform asynchronous operations, dispatch other actions or interact with the state.\\nAn example of its use\\n\\nWhere is it used?\\n\\n- The logging of the user\'s actions\\n- Navigation after a successful action (a redirect after a login, for example)\\n- Synchronization with localStorage or IndexedDB\\n- A call to an API without creating a thunk\\nConclusion\\ncreateListenerMiddleware is a convenient tool for handling side effects in Redux when the full power of sagas or thunks is not required. It is especially useful for simple scenarios, where you have to respond to actions without changing the reducers\' logic.","import { createListenerMiddleware, isAnyOf } from \'@reduxjs/toolkit\';\\nimport { addTodo, removeTodo } from \'./todosSlice\';\\n\\nconst listenerMiddleware = createListenerMiddleware();\\n\\nlistenerMiddleware.startListening({\\n  matcher: isAnyOf(addTodo, removeTodo),\\n  effect: async (action, listenerApi) => {\\n    console.log(\'A todo has been changed:\', action);\\n    // Other actions can be dispatched\\n    listenerApi.dispatch({ type: \'log/action\', payload: action.type });\\n  },\\n});\\n\\nexport default listenerMiddleware;"],[2902,"Prop drilling arises when data is passed through several levels of components, even if the intermediate components do not use it. That makes the code cumbersome and hard to maintain. State managers (Redux, Zustand, for example) or the Context API let you hold global state and provide it directly to the components that need it, bypassing the intermediate links.","The problem of prop drilling\\nIn React, data is passed from a parent component to its children through props. When an application grows, a situation arises in which props have to be passed through several levels of nesting, although the intermediate components do not use them. That is called prop drilling. Such an approach makes refactoring more complex, increases the coupling of the components and makes the code less readable.\\nHow state managers solve that problem\\nState managers (Redux, MobX, Zustand, for example) or the built-in Context API provide a mechanism for holding global state, available to any component without being passed explicitly through props. A component can subscribe to the part of the state it needs and receive the updates automatically.\\nAn example with the Context API\\n\\nIn this example Toolbar receives no props, and ThemedButton uses the context directly. That eliminates prop drilling.\\nConclusion\\nState managers and the Context API solve the problem of prop drilling, making the application\'s architecture simpler and the code more maintainable. They are worth applying when the data is used by many components at different levels of nesting, so as to avoid the superfluous passing of props.","import React, { createContext, useContext, useState } from \'react\';\\n\\nconst ThemeContext = createContext();\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [theme, setTheme] = useState(\'light\');\\n  return (\\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value={{ theme, setTheme }}>\\n      <Toolbar />\\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Toolbar() {\\n  return <ThemedButton />;\\n}\\n\\nfunction ThemedButton() {\\n  const { theme, setTheme } = useContext(ThemeContext);\\n  return (\\n    <button onClick={() => setTheme(theme === \'light\' ? \'dark\' : \'light\')}>\\n      Current theme: {theme}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n}"],[2903,"The user or the code calls dispatch(action). Redux passes the action and the current state into the reducer. The reducer returns a new state. The store saves it and notifies its subscribers. React repaints the components connected through connect or useSelector.","Redux\'s life cycle\\nRedux is a predictable state container for JavaScript applications. The whole cycle begins with the call of the dispatch function with an action object, which contains a type and optional data. Next Redux passes the current state and the action into the root reducer, which decides how to change the state. A reducer has to be a pure function: it does not change the original state but returns a new object. After that the store updates its internal state and calls every subscriber registered through subscribe. In a React application the subscription is carried out through react-redux: connect or the useSelector/useDispatch hooks. When the store gives notice of a change, React starts a re-render of the components that depend on the part of the state that has changed.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn understanding of this cycle is necessary for debugging, for optimizing the performance and for designing the architecture of Redux applications properly. Use this approach when the centralized management of state with predictable changes is required.","// The action creator\\nconst increment = () => ({ type: \'INCREMENT\' });\\n\\n// The reducer\\nconst counter = (state = 0, action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case \'INCREMENT\':\\n      return state + 1;\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n};\\n\\n// The store\\nconst store = createStore(counter);\\n\\n// The dispatch\\nstore.dispatch(increment());\\nconsole.log(store.getState()); // 1"],[2904,"Redux Observable uses epics in order to handle side effects. An epic is a function that takes a stream of actions and returns a stream of new actions. By means of RxJS operators such as mergeMap, switchMap or debounceTime you can manage asynchronous operations — HTTP requests or timers, for example. That lets you separate the effect logic from the reducers and the components.","What are Redux Observable and epics?\\nRedux Observable is middleware for Redux, based on the RxJS library. It lets you handle side effects by means of epics. An epic is a function that takes a stream of all of the actions (actions$) and returns a stream of new actions. That is similar to redux-saga, but it uses reactive programming.\\nHow do epics work?\\nAn epic subscribes to the stream of actions, filters the ones it needs, performs an asynchronous operation (a request to an API, for example) and dispatches a new action with the result. All of that is done by means of RxJS operators such as `filter`, `mergeMap`, `catchError` and others.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhere is it used?\\nRedux Observable is useful in complex applications where the fine management of asynchronous streams, the cancellation of requests, debouncing or the combination of several sources of data is required. It is especially good for real-time applications (chats, notifications) or for complex interaction logic.\\nConclusion: use Redux Observable if your application already uses RxJS or requires a reactive approach to the management of side effects. For simple cases redux-thunk will do.","import { ofType } from \'redux-observable\';\\nimport { ajax } from \'rxjs/ajax\';\\nimport { map, mergeMap, catchError } from \'rxjs/operators\';\\nimport { of } from \'rxjs\';\\n\\nconst fetchUserEpic = (action$) =>\\n  action$.pipe(\\n    ofType(\'FETCH_USER\'),\\n    mergeMap((action) =>\\n      ajax.getJSON(`/api/users/${action.payload}`).pipe(\\n        map((response) => ({\\n          type: \'FETCH_USER_SUCCESS\',\\n          payload: response,\\n        })),\\n        catchError((error) =>\\n          of({\\n            type: \'FETCH_USER_FAILURE\',\\n            payload: error.message,\\n          })\\n        )\\n      )\\n    )\\n  );"],[2905,"In React, local state, the Context API and libraries such as Redux, MobX and Zustand are used for managing state. Redux is based on the Flux architecture: a single store, immutable state, pure reducer functions and a one-directional flow of data. Actions are dispatched, the reducers create a new state, and the components subscribe to the changes.","The approaches to state management in React\\nIn React applications the state can be local (useState, useReducer) or global. For global state, the Context API built into React is used, or specialized libraries: Redux, MobX, Zustand, Recoil, Jotai. The choice depends on the complexity of the application: for simple projects the Context API is enough; for large ones, Redux or MobX.\\nRedux\'s architecture\\nRedux implements the Flux pattern with a single source of truth — the store. The state is read-only; the changes happen by way of pure reducer functions. The flow of data is one-directional: a component dispatches an action, the action reaches a reducer, the reducer creates a new state, and the store notifies its subscribers.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nRedux suits large applications with a multitude of interrelated pieces of state, where predictability and debugging matter. For small projects it is better to use the Context API or lighter libraries such as Zustand.","// The action types\\nconst ADD_TODO = \'ADD_TODO\';\\n\\n// The action creator\\nconst addTodo = (text) => ({\\n  type: ADD_TODO,\\n  payload: text\\n});\\n\\n// The reducer\\nconst todosReducer = (state = [], action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case ADD_TODO:\\n      return [...state, { text: action.payload, completed: false }];\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n};\\n\\n// The store\\nimport { createStore } from \'redux\';\\nconst store = createStore(todosReducer);\\n\\n// The dispatch\\nstore.dispatch(addTodo(\'Learn Redux\'));"],[2906,"State managers (Redux, for example) are needed for the centralized management of an application\'s complex state, especially when the data is used by many components. The Context API solves a similar task, but it is less performant under frequent updates, since it causes a re-render of every consumer. State managers offer a more predictable update and tools for debugging.","What are state managers for?\\nIn React the state is normally local to a component. When an application grows, the need arises to share the state between many components, often located far from one another in the tree. State managers such as Redux, MobX or Zustand provide a single store and strict rules for changing it, which makes the flow of data predictable and makes debugging easier.\\nHow does Context differ?\\nThe Context API is React\'s built-in mechanism for passing data without prop drilling. It is simple to use, but it has disadvantages: when a context\'s value changes, every component that consumes it is re-rendered, even if they do not use the part that has changed. That can lead to problems with performance in large applications.\\nAn example of the comparison\\nLet us look at an application with a shopping cart. Using Context:\\n\\nAny component using `useContext(CartContext)` will be re-rendered on every addition of a product, even if it shows only the total sum.\\nWith Redux:\\n\\nHere `TotalPrice` will be re-rendered only if the computed sum changes, rather than on any change of the cart.\\nConclusion\\nUse Context for simple global state (the theme, the language) that changes rarely. For complex, frequently updated state with a multitude of consumers, choose a state manager — that will give better performance and predictability.","const CartContext = React.createContext();\\n\\nfunction CartProvider({ children }) {\\n  const [items, setItems] = useState([]);\\n  const addItem = (item) => setItems(prev => [...prev, item]);\\n  return (\\n    <CartContext.Provider value={{ items, addItem }}>\\n      {children}\\n    </CartContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// The slice\\nconst cartSlice = createSlice({\\n  name: \'cart\',\\n  initialState: { items: [] },\\n  reducers: {\\n    addItem: (state, action) => { state.items.push(action.payload); },\\n  },\\n});\\n\\n// The component\\nfunction TotalPrice() {\\n  const total = useSelector(state =>\\n    state.cart.items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price, 0)\\n  );\\n  return <div>Total: {total}</div>;\\n}"],[2907,"Actions are objects describing an intention to change the state — { type: \'ADD_TODO\', payload: \'buy milk\' }, for example. A reducer is a pure function that takes the current state and an action and returns a new state. A reducer does not change the original state but creates a new one. Together they form a predictable flow of data: a component calls an action, Redux passes it into the reducer, and the state is updated.","The basics of Redux: actions and reducers\\nIn React, Redux is often used for managing complex state. The key concepts are actions and reducers. Actions are simple objects, which describe what has happened in the application. They contain a mandatory `type` field (an identifier string) and an optional `payload` (the data). For example, `{ type: \'INCREMENT\', payload: 1 }`.\\nA reducer is a pure function, which determines how the state changes in response to an action. It takes two arguments: the current state and the action, and returns a new state. It matters that a reducer does not mutate the original state but creates a copy of it with the changes. That makes the state predictable and easy to test.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example the action creator returns an action object, which is then passed into the reducer through `dispatch`. The reducer handles the action and returns a new state. Such an approach lets you manage the state centrally and track the changes easily.\\nWhere it is used\\nRedux is used in large React applications, where many components exchange data. It is especially useful when working with server-side data, caching, complex forms and collaborative editing. For simple applications, however, the built-in `useState` or `useReducer` will do.\\nIn summary: actions and reducers are the foundation of Redux. They ensure the predictable updating of the state and make debugging and testing easier. Apply Redux when the state becomes complex and requires global access from many components.","// The action creator\\nconst increment = (amount) => ({\\n  type: \'INCREMENT\',\\n  payload: amount\\n});\\n\\n// The reducer\\nconst counterReducer = (state = 0, action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case \'INCREMENT\':\\n      return state + action.payload;\\n    case \'DECREMENT\':\\n      return state - action.payload;\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n};\\n\\n// Using it in the store\\nconst store = createStore(counterReducer);\\nstore.dispatch(increment(5));\\nconsole.log(store.getState()); // 5"],[2908,"Redux is not needed when an application\'s state is simple and local, when there is no complex exchange of data between the components, or when the project is small. In such cases useState, useReducer or the Context API is enough. Redux is justified at large scales, with complex logic and where predictable state is necessary.","When Redux is superfluous\\nRedux is a powerful tool, but using it unnecessarily makes the code more complex and increases the development time. The main signal that Redux is not needed is the simplicity of the state. If the data is used only inside one component or a few neighbouring ones, it is better to make do with local state through `useState` or `useReducer`.\\nRedux is also not needed when the project is small or is a prototype. Adding Redux requires the configuration of a store, reducers and actions and the connection of a provider, which creates a lot of boilerplate code. For simple applications that is an unjustified cost.\\nAlternatives to Redux\\n\\n- useState — for a component\'s local state.\\n- useReducer — for more complex logic inside a component.\\n- The Context API — for passing data through the tree of components without props, if the updates are not frequent.\\n- React Query / SWR — for managing server state and caching.\\nAn example: when Redux is not needed\\n\\nHere there is no sense in bringing Redux in — the state is isolated and is not used by other components.\\nWhen Redux really is useful\\n\\n- A large application with a multitude of components sharing a common state.\\n- Complex state-update logic (a shopping cart with a multitude of operations, for example).\\n- The need for debugging and time travel (time-travel debugging).\\n- Team development, where uniformity and predictability matter.\\nConclusion: Redux is worth applying only when the complexity of managing the state exceeds the cost of introducing it. For most small and medium projects React\'s built-in means are enough, which makes the code simpler and faster to develop.","// A simple application: a login form\\nfunction LoginForm() {\\n  const [email, setEmail] = useState(\'\');\\n  const [password, setPassword] = useState(\'\');\\n  // Local state is enough\\n  return <input value={email} onChange={e => setEmail(e.target.value)} />;\\n}"],[2909,"A component connected to Redux is re-rendered when the part of the state it has selected changes. Redux notifies its subscribers through store.subscribe(), and React-Redux uses context and hooks (useSelector) or the connect HOC in order to subscribe to the changes. When the state changes, the component receives the new data and is repainted.","How Redux notifies the components\\nRedux holds the state in a single store. When a dispatch(action) happens, the reducer creates a new state object. After that the store calls every subscriber registered through the `store.subscribe()` method. React-Redux subscribes the connected components to the store automatically, using that mechanism.\\nThe role of React-Redux\\nThe React-Redux library provides two main ways of connecting: the `connect` HOC and the `useSelector`/`useDispatch` hooks. Both use React\'s context in order to access the store and subscribe to the changes. When the state changes, React-Redux compares the data selected against the previous data (through the selector function) and, if it has changed, causes a re-render of the component.\\nAn example with useSelector\\n\\nHere `useSelector` subscribes to the store. On every change of the state it runs the selector and compares the result with the previous one. If the result differs, the component is re-rendered.\\nWhy a re-render happens only when the data selected changes\\nReact-Redux uses strict comparison (===) by default. If the selector returns a new object every time, that can cause superfluous re-renders. For optimization you can use `shallowEqual` or the memoization of the selectors.\\nConclusion\\nAn understanding of this mechanism helps you avoid superfluous re-renders and write effective React applications with Redux. Use selectors that return stable references, and apply memoization where necessary.","import { useSelector } from \'react-redux\';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const count = useSelector(state => state.counter.value);\\n  return <div>{count}</div>;\\n}"],[2910,"Yes, you can call another module\'s selector, if it takes the whole state or part of it. It is better organized by way of the composition of selectors: export the base selectors from a module and use them in other modules, passing in the slice of state needed. That preserves the encapsulation and makes testing easier.","An introduction\\nIn Redux, selectors are pure functions that take the state and return derived data. They help you avoid duplicating logic and make testing easier. The question of calling one module\'s selector from another arises when designing large applications, where the state is broken into several slices.\\nThe main concept\\nSelectors can be of two kinds: global (they take the whole state) and local (they take only their own slice). If a module\'s selector is written as a local one, it expects only its own sub-object of the state as its input. To use it in another module, you have to pass in the corresponding slice from the global state. That is done by way of composition: in the other module a new selector is created, which first extracts the slice needed and then calls the local selector.\\nAn example\\nSuppose there is a `users` module with a `selectUserById` selector, which takes the `state.users` slice and an id:\\n\\nIn the `posts` module a post\'s author has to be obtained. We can import that selector and pass it the slice from the global state:\\n\\nHere `selectPostAuthor` uses the selector from the other module, passing it `state.users`. That is possible because the `selectUserById` selector does not depend on the global state but works with the slice passed in.\\nThe best practices\\n\\n- Export the local selectors from every module, so that they can be reused.\\n- Avoid creating selectors that address the global state directly inside a module — that makes testing and reuse more complex.\\n- Use the composition of selectors in order to create new selectors on the basis of existing ones.\\n- For complex dependencies you can use the `reselect` library, which supports memoization and makes composition easier.\\nConclusion\\nCalling another module\'s selector is normal practice, if the selectors are written as pure functions taking a slice of the state. Organize the modules so that each one exports its own local selectors, and combine them in other modules by way of passing in the slice needed. That preserves the cleanliness of the architecture and makes maintaining the code easier.","// users/selectors.js\\nexport const selectUserById = (usersState, id) =>\\n  usersState.entities[id];\\n\\n// posts/selectors.js\\nimport { selectUserById } from \'../users/selectors\';\\n\\nexport const selectPostAuthor = (state, postId) => {\\n  const post = state.posts.entities[postId];\\n  return selectUserById(state.users, post.authorId);\\n};"],[2911,"To avoid excessive nesting in a Redux store, use the normalization of the data: hold the entities separately by type (users, posts, for example) in the form of objects with identifier keys, and express the relations between them through IDs. That makes updating and searching easier and prevents duplication. Also avoid deep trees, by grouping the data by domain and using selectors for the selection.","The problem of excessive nesting\\nWhen the data in a Redux store is held in the form of deeply nested objects (a user object contains an array of their posts, every post contains comments and so on, for example), problems arise: it is hard to update the nested fields without mutations, it is difficult to reuse the data, and the risk of duplication and of going out of sync increases. If one and the same post appears in two different places, for example, changing it will require an update in each place.\\nNormalization as the solution\\nNormalization is the approach in which the data is held as a flat structure, where every entity (a user, a post, a comment) is held separately in its own collection and the relations between them are expressed through identifiers. Instead of a nested user object with posts, for example, we hold a users object, where the key is the user\'s ID, and a posts object, where the key is the post\'s ID and the post has an authorId field. That lets you update the entities easily, avoid duplication and makes the selection of data easier.\\nA practical example\\nLet us look at the structure of a blog. Instead of:\\n\\nWe use a normalized structure:\\n\\nNow the updating of a comment requires only a change in comments rather than in a deep nesting. To select the data, use selectors that assemble the relations needed.\\nThe rules of organization\\n\\n- Hold every entity in a separate collection (users, posts, comments, for example).\\n- Use objects with identifier keys rather than arrays, for fast access.\\n- Reference other entities through their IDs rather than nested objects.\\n- Group the data by domain (auth, cart, catalog, for example) for a logical structure.\\n- Avoid a nesting deeper than 2-3 levels; if it is needed, consider normalization.\\nConclusion\\nThe normalization of a Redux store is the standard approach for managing complex data. It makes updating easier, prevents duplication and makes the state predictable. Apply it when you have related entities that change or are reused often. That is especially useful in large applications with a large number of interrelations.","{\\n  user: {\\n    id: 1,\\n    name: \'Alice\',\\n    posts: [\\n      { id: 101, title: \'Hello\', comments: [{ id: 501, text: \'Nice\' }] }\\n    ]\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n{\\n  users: { 1: { id: 1, name: \'Alice\' } },\\n  posts: { 101: { id: 101, title: \'Hello\', authorId: 1 } },\\n  comments: { 501: { id: 501, text: \'Nice\', postId: 101 } }\\n}"],[2912,"No, a deep copy is not required. It is enough to create a new object at the top level, preserving the references to the nested objects that have not changed. That is called a shallow copy. Redux compares references, so it matters to change only the parts of the state that really have changed.","The main idea\\nIn Redux the state is considered immutable. That means you cannot mutate the existing state object but have to create a new object that reflects the changes. That does not mean, however, that the whole state has to be deep-copied in its entirety. It is enough to create a new object at the top level and preserve the references to the nested objects that have not changed.\\nWhy is a deep copy superfluous?\\nA deep copy creates new objects for all of the nested levels, which requires additional memory and time. Redux uses the comparison of references in order to determine the changes: if the reference to an object has changed, Redux considers that the state has changed. When updating a single field, therefore, it is enough to create a new object for the level at which the change happened and preserve the references to the remaining parts.\\nAn example\\nSuppose we have the state:\\n\\nIf we want to update the user\'s age, we can do it like this:\\n\\nHere we have created a new object for the top level and a new object for user, but the reference to posts has stayed the same. That is a shallow copy, and it is enough for Redux.\\nWhen might a deep copy be needed?\\nA deep copy can be useful if you want to isolate the state from external mutations entirely — when working with libraries that mutate objects, for example. In the context of Redux, however, it is not required and can lead to superfluous performance costs.\\nConclusion\\nWhen updating a single field in Redux state, use a shallow copy: create a new object at every level where a change happens, and preserve the references to the parts that have not changed. That is effective and corresponds to Redux\'s principles.","const state = {\\n  user: {\\n    name: \'Alice\',\\n    age: 30\\n  },\\n  posts: []\\n};\\n\\nconst newState = {\\n  ...state,\\n  user: {\\n    ...state.user,\\n    age: 31\\n  }\\n};"],[2913,"In Redux the data moves strictly in one direction. A component calls dispatch(action), the action reaches a reducer, which returns a new state. The store saves the state and notifies the subscribed components, which are repainted with the new data. That makes the flow predictable and easy to track.","The main idea\\nRedux is a library for managing state, which uses a one-directional flow of data. That means the data always moves around a single circle: component → action → reducer → store → component. Such an approach makes debugging easier and makes the application\'s behaviour predictable.\\nThe complete cycle\\n\\n- A component calls `dispatch(action)` — that is the only way to change the state.\\n- An action is a simple object with a `type` field and, optionally, a `payload`.\\n- A reducer is a pure function, which takes the current state and the action and returns a new state.\\n- The store saves the new state and notifies every subscriber through `subscribe`.\\n- The components connected through `connect` or `useSelector` receive the updated data and are repainted.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhy that matters\\nThe one-directional flow makes tracking the changes easier: you always know where the data came from and how it changed. That is especially useful in large applications, where many components exchange state. Redux also supports middleware (Redux Thunk or Saga, for example) for asynchronous actions, but the basic flow stays unchanged.\\nIn summary: Redux is worth applying when an application\'s state becomes complex and requires predictable management. It is especially useful in large projects with a multitude of interacting components, where the centralized holding of the data and clear logic for changing it matter.","// The action creator\\nconst addTodo = (text) => ({\\n  type: \'ADD_TODO\',\\n  payload: text\\n});\\n\\n// The reducer\\nconst todosReducer = (state = [], action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case \'ADD_TODO\':\\n      return [...state, { text: action.payload, done: false }];\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n};\\n\\n// The component\\nconst TodoInput = ({ dispatch }) => {\\n  const handleSubmit = (e) => {\\n    e.preventDefault();\\n    dispatch(addTodo(inputValue));\\n  };\\n  return <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>...</form>;\\n};\\n\\n// The connection\\nconst mapDispatchToProps = { addTodo };\\nexport default connect(null, mapDispatchToProps)(TodoInput);"],[2914,"No, the data must not be mutated in a reducer. Redux requires the state to be updated immutably, by returning a new object. For arrays, use the spread operator, map, filter, concat. For objects, the spread operator or Object.assign. That guarantees predictability and the correct working of React\'s repaints.","Why immutability is critical in Redux\\nRedux is built on the principle that the state is an immutable object. Every time you want to change the state, you create a new object rather than modifying the old one. That lets Redux track the changes by the comparison of references, which makes the application predictable and makes debugging easier. If you mutate the state directly, React may not detect the change and the UI will not update.\\nHow to update arrays properly\\nFor arrays, use the methods that return a new array: `map`, `filter`, `concat`, or the spread operator. To add an element, for example:\\n\\nTo remove an element, use `filter`:\\n\\nTo update a particular element, `map`:\\n\\nHow to update objects properly\\nFor objects, use the spread operator or `Object.assign`. For example:\\n\\nIf the object is nested, update every level separately, so as not to lose the other fields.\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- Never use `push`, `splice` or the direct assignment of properties.\\n- For complex structures you can use libraries of the Immer kind, which let you write mutable code but create immutable updates automatically.\\n- Remember that immutability is not only a rule of Redux but good practice for any complex state.\\nConclusion: immutability in Redux is a fundamental requirement, which ensures predictability and performance. Always return a new state object, using the spread operator or methods that return new arrays. To make working with deep structures easier, Immer can be applied.","case \'ADD_TODO\':\\n  return {\\n    ...state,\\n    todos: [...state.todos, action.payload]\\n  };\\n\\ncase \'REMOVE_TODO\':\\n  return {\\n    ...state,\\n    todos: state.todos.filter(todo => todo.id !== action.payload)\\n  };\\n\\ncase \'TOGGLE_TODO\':\\n  return {\\n    ...state,\\n    todos: state.todos.map(todo =>\\n      todo.id === action.payload ? { ...todo, completed: !todo.completed } : todo\\n    )\\n  };\\n\\ncase \'UPDATE_USER\':\\n  return {\\n    ...state,\\n    user: {\\n      ...state.user,\\n      name: action.payload.name\\n    }\\n  };"],[2915,"Holding heterogeneous data in a single global Redux store is a widespread practice, but it calls for careful structuring. Redux presupposes one store for all of the state, which makes debugging and synchronization easier; as an application grows, however, it can lead to excessive coupling and complexity. It is recommended to divide the data into slices (reducers) and to use selectors in order to isolate the logic.","The concept of a single store in Redux\\nRedux, by its philosophy, uses a single global store, which holds all of an application\'s state. That lets you have a single source of truth, makes debugging easier (by means of Redux DevTools, for example) and makes the state predictable. Holding heterogeneous data such as a user\'s profile and the shopping cart in one store is not an error, however — it is the standard approach. The problems arise when the store\'s structure is not organized properly.\\nThe effect on the architecture\\nIf all of the data lies in one store without a clear division, that can lead to the following problems: the complexity of maintenance, difficulties with scaling, superfluous repaints of the components and coupling between unrelated modules. To avoid that, Redux proposes breaking the state into slices by means of the combination of reducers. Every slice is responsible for its own domain area — `user` and `cart`, for example. That preserves the single store but makes it structured.\\nA practical example\\n\\nSuch an approach lets every module work only with its own part of the store, and the selectors help you isolate the data. The cart component uses `state.cart`, for example, and the profile `state.user`.\\nWhen it is justified\\nA single store is justified for medium and large applications, where the centralized management of state matters. For very large projects you can consider alternatives such as modular state (Zustand, for example), but Redux remains a reliable choice thanks to its predictability and its tools.\\nIn summary: holding heterogeneous data in one Redux store is good practice, if the state is structured properly through slices and selectors. That makes the architecture simpler and scalable and makes debugging easier, but it calls for discipline in the organization of the code.","// store.js\\nimport { combineReducers, createStore } from \'redux\';\\nimport userReducer from \'./userReducer\';\\nimport cartReducer from \'./cartReducer\';\\n\\nconst rootReducer = combineReducers({\\n  user: userReducer,\\n  cart: cartReducer\\n});\\n\\nconst store = createStore(rootReducer);\\n// Now the state is: { user: {...}, cart: {...} }"],[2916,"For the exchange of data between unrelated components in React, global state (Redux, the Context API), the lifting-state-up pattern or an event bus is used. Lifting the state up suits components that are close together, Context medium applications, Redux complex ones. An event bus (through an EventEmitter, for example) lets them communicate without a common parent, but makes debugging more complex.","An introduction to the problem\\nIn React, data is normally passed from the top down through props. When components are not related directly (they have no common parent), the standard flow of data becomes inconvenient. The need arises for mechanisms that let components exchange information without an explicit hierarchy.\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- Lifting State Up: if the components have a common parent, the state is moved out into it and passed down through props. It is the simplest way for small hierarchies.\\n- The Context API: a mechanism built into React for passing data through the tree of components without props at every level. Suits themes, authorization, language settings.\\n- Global state (Redux, Zustand, MobX): external libraries, which hold the state in a single store and let any component subscribe to the changes. Used in large applications with frequent updates.\\n- An Event Bus: a global object is created, which emits and listens for events. The components can communicate directly, but that violates the one-directional flow of data and makes testing more complex.\\nAn example with the Context API\\n\\nAn example with Redux (briefly)\\n\\nWhen to choose which\\n\\n- For simple cases with a common parent — lifting the state up.\\n- For medium applications with several levels — the Context API.\\n- For complex applications with a large number of interactions — Redux or its analogues.\\n- An event bus — only if there is no other way out, since it makes the flow of data unpredictable.\\nConclusion\\nThe choice of method depends on the scale of the application and on the frequency of the updates. Begin with lifting the state up, move on to Context where necessary, and use Redux only when the state becomes really complex. That will ensure the maintainability and the predictability of the code.","const DataContext = React.createContext();\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [data, setData] = React.useState(\'\');\\n  return (\\n    <DataContext.Provider value={{ data, setData }}>\\n      <ComponentA />\\n      <ComponentB />\\n    </DataContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction ComponentA() {\\n  const { setData } = React.useContext(DataContext);\\n  return <button onClick={() => setData(\'Hello\')}>Send</button>;\\n}\\n\\nfunction ComponentB() {\\n  const { data } = React.useContext(DataContext);\\n  return <p>Received: {data}</p>;\\n}\\n\\n// store.js\\nimport { createStore } from \'redux\';\\nconst reducer = (state = {}, action) => {\\n  if (action.type === \'SET\') return { ...state, data: action.payload };\\n  return state;\\n};\\nexport const store = createStore(reducer);\\n\\n// ComponentA\\nstore.dispatch({ type: \'SET\', payload: \'Hello\' });\\n\\n// ComponentB\\nstore.subscribe(() => console.log(store.getState().data));"],[2917,"A developer creates a branch, makes the changes and opens a merge request. The other developers review the code, leave comments and suggest corrections. Once it has been approved, the code is merged into the main branch. Such a process helps keep the quality of the code under control.","A merge request is the mechanism for introducing changes into a project\'s main code by way of a review.\\n\\nThe typical process\\n\\n- A feature branch is created\\n\\n- The changes are made and a commit is performed\\n- A push to the remote repository\\n\\n- A Merge Request is created\\n- It goes through code review\\n- Once it has been approved, the merge is performed\\n\\nWhat is reviewed\\nAt review, attention is paid to:\\n- the architecture\\n- the readability\\n- the error handling\\n- the tests\\n\\nAdditional checks\\nAn MR is often checked automatically by:\\n- linters\\n- tests\\n- the build\\n\\nConclusion\\nA merge request is the main mechanism for introducing changes safely, which reduces the risk of errors and improves the quality of the code.","\\ngit checkout -b feature/login\\n\\ngit push origin feature/login"],[2918,"If you do not use linters and pre-commit hooks, style errors, potential bugs and technical debt build up in the codebase quickly. Different developers start writing code in different ways, which makes it harder to read and to maintain. Errors that could have been caught automatically make it into the repository and into CI. The result is that the time spent on code review increases and the risk of defects in production grows.","A definition\\nA linter is a static-analysis tool that checks code automatically for errors, violations of style and potential problems. A pre-commit hook is a script that runs before a commit and blocks it if the rules it has been given are violated.\\n\\nThe main problems in their absence\\nThe absence of automatic checks leads to a number of systemic consequences:\\n- Unstable code quality\\n- different formatting styles\\n- inconsistent approaches to naming\\n- difficulty in reading and maintaining it\\n\\n- A growth in the number of simple errors\\n- unused variables\\n- typos\\n- a forgotten `await` or `return`\\n\\n- Overloaded code reviews\\n- the reviewers spend their time on style rather than on logic\\n- the time it takes to accept a change increases\\n\\n- Problems in CI/CD\\n- errors are detected too late\\n- builds fail because of something that could have been caught locally\\n\\nA practical example\\nWithout a linter, code like this can reach the repository without comment:\\n\\nWith a linter the problem will be detected automatically:\\n- a violation of `snake_case`\\n- the potential shadowing of a built-in name\\n\\nConclusion\\nLinters and pre-commit hooks let you catch cheap errors as early as possible, reduce the load on the review and stabilize the quality of the code across the team.","\\ndef getUser(id):\\n    user = db.get(id)\\n    return user"],[2919,"Kanban is a methodology that visualizes the working process on a board with columns (\\"To Do\\", \\"In Progress\\", \\"Review\\", \\"Done\\", for example). Each task is represented by a card, which moves across the columns. Code review is the process in which colleagues review code before it is merged into the main branch. In Kanban a task\'s card moves into the \\"Review\\" column once the development has finished, where another developer checks the code for conformity to the standards, the absence of errors and readability. After a successful review the card moves to \\"Done\\". This process ensures a continuous flow of work and raises the quality of the code.","Kanban is an agile methodology for managing work, which focuses on visualizing the flow of tasks and limiting the work in progress (WIP). In software development it is often implemented by means of a physical or digital board divided into columns that reflect the stages of the process. The typical columns: \\"Backlog\\", \\"To Do\\", \\"In Progress\\", \\"Review\\", \\"Testing\\" and \\"Done\\". Each task or user story is represented by a card, which moves from left to right as it progresses.\\n\\nIntegrating code review into the Kanban flow\\nCode review is a mandatory quality-control stage built into the working process. Once a developer has finished working on a task (implementing a feature or fixing a bug, for example), they create a pull request (or merge request) in the version-control system (GitHub or GitLab, for example). At that moment the task\'s card on the Kanban board moves from the \\"In Progress\\" column into the \\"Review\\" column. That signals to the team that the code is ready to be reviewed.\\n\\nHow the review process goes\\n\\n- Assigning a reviewer: the reviewer is often assigned automatically or chosen from the members of the team familiar with the task\'s context.\\n- Reviewing the code: the reviewer studies the changes — the correctness of the logic, the conformity to the coding standards, the presence of tests, the readability and the potential vulnerabilities.\\n- Feedback: the reviewer leaves comments directly in the pull request\'s interface. They can be blocking ones (requiring a correction) or informational ones (advice).\\n- Iteration: the author makes the necessary corrections, commits them, and the code becomes available for review again. This cycle can repeat.\\n- Merging: once it has been approved by the reviewer or by the required number of colleagues, the code is merged into the main branch (main, for example). The task\'s card then moves into the \\"Done\\" column or, if there is a testing stage, into \\"Testing\\".\\n\\nA practical example of the workflow in Git\\n\\nSuch an approach ensures transparency: the whole team sees which tasks are at review and can judge the load. Limiting the WIP in the \\"Review\\" column helps you avoid bottlenecks and speeds the feedback up.\\n\\nConclusion: using Kanban and code review together creates a structured, visible and iterative development process, which minimizes errors, spreads knowledge across the team and sustains a steady flow of releases of quality code. That is especially useful in teams aiming for continuous delivery and a high degree of collaboration.","# 1. The developer creates a branch for the task from main\\ngit checkout -b feature/add-login-button\\n\\n# 2. Writes the code, commits the changes\\ngit add .\\ngit commit -m \\"Add login button component\\"\\n\\n# 3. Pushes the branch to the remote repository\\ngit push origin feature/add-login-button\\n\\n# 4. Creates a Pull Request in GitHub/GitLab.\\n#    In the PR\'s description, references the task (a JIRA ticket, for example).\\n#    The card in Kanban (in Jira, for example) moves to \\"Review\\" automatically.\\n\\n# 5. A colleague carries out the review, leaves comments.\\n# 6. The developer makes corrections, pushes new commits.\\n# 7. After the approve, the PR is merged.\\n# 8. The task\'s card moves to \\"Done\\" automatically."],[2920,"Git Flow is a popular branching model for Git. It uses the main branches main (for production) and develop (for development), plus the supporting ones: feature (for new functionality), release (for preparing a release) and hotfix (for urgent fixes). This structure helps you organize the process, isolate changes and ensure the stability of the main codebase. Teams often adapt it to their own needs, using simplified variants such as GitHub Flow, for example.","Git Flow is a branching model proposed by Vincent Driessen, which defines strict rules for creating and merging branches in Git. It is intended for projects with a clear release cycle and helps you manage parallel development, stabilization and maintenance.\\n\\nThe key branches\\n\\n- main (master): contains only stable, production-ready code. Every commit here is a new release.\\n- develop: the main branch for integrating new functionality. Finished feature branches are merged in here.\\n- feature/*: created off develop in order to develop new functionality. Once finished, they are merged back into develop.\\n- release/*: created off develop once enough changes have built up for a new release. The final testing and bug fixing happens here. Once it is ready, it is merged both into main (with a version tag) and into develop (so as to carry the fixes across).\\n- hotfix/*: created off main for an urgent fix in production. Once fixed, they are merged both into main (a new patch release) and into develop.\\n\\nAn example of the working process\\n\\nWhere it is used\\nGit Flow suits projects with long-lived releases well (desktop applications or corporate software, for example), where there are clear versions and a need to maintain several versions at once. For services with continuous deployment (CI/CD), simpler models are often used, such as GitHub Flow (one main branch, feature branches merged through a pull request) or GitLab Flow (with environments).\\n\\nConclusion: Git Flow is worth applying in teams where strict release discipline, the isolation of the development stages and support for several versions of the product matter. For small teams or services with frequent deploys it can be excessive.","# Creating a feature branch for a new task\\ngit checkout develop\\ngit checkout -b feature/user-authentication\\n\\n# Working on the task, commits...\\ngit add .\\ngit commit -m \\"Add login form\\"\\n\\n# Finishing the feature: merging into develop\\ngit checkout develop\\ngit merge --no-ff feature/user-authentication\\ngit branch -d feature/user-authentication\\n\\n# When the release is ready, we create a release branch\\ngit checkout -b release/v1.2.0 develop\\n# Testing, fixing bugs right in this branch...\\n\\n# Finishing the release: merging into main and develop\\ngit checkout main\\ngit merge --no-ff release/v1.2.0\\ngit tag -a v1.2.0 -m \\"Release version 1.2.0\\"\\ngit checkout develop\\ngit merge --no-ff release/v1.2.0\\ngit branch -d release/v1.2.0\\n\\n# An urgent hotfix for production\\ngit checkout main\\ngit checkout -b hotfix/critical-security-patch\\n# The fix...\\ngit commit -m \\"Fix security vulnerability\\"\\ngit checkout main\\ngit merge --no-ff hotfix/critical-security-patch\\ngit tag -a v1.2.1 -m \\"Hotfix release 1.2.1\\"\\ngit checkout develop\\ngit merge --no-ff hotfix/critical-security-patch\\ngit branch -d hotfix/critical-security-patch"],[2921,"Reproducing a bug is the process of creating the conditions under which the error arises consistently. First you have to gather the exact steps to reproduce it from the user or from the logs. Then you have to isolate the environment: check the versions of the software, the configuration, the data. It matters to minimize the number of steps down to the bare essentials. After that you should repeat those steps in a controlled environment, in order to make sure the bug shows itself. Without reproducing it, a fix is impossible.","Reproducing a bug is a fundamental skill in development, which turns a vague description of a problem into a concrete, manageable task. Without consistent reproduction a developer cannot confirm the error exists, understand its cause or verify a fix.\\n\\nThe key steps of the process\\n\\n- Gathering the information: obtain the most detailed description you can from the user or from the monitoring system. What exactly happened? What were the expectations? At what moment did the error appear?\\n- Determining the steps: compose an exact, step-by-step sequence of actions leading to the error. The ideal steps should be minimal and independent of the particular user.\\n- Recreating the environment: an error can depend on a multitude of factors — the version of the browser or the OS, particular data in the database, the state of the cache, the access rights. You have to repeat that environment as precisely as you can.\\n- Isolation: try to simplify the scenario. Can the superfluous steps be removed? Does the error show itself on other data or in another configuration? That helps you narrow the range of potential causes down.\\n\\nA practical example\\nImagine that in a web application a request to an API fails under certain conditions. Here is what the process of reproducing it might look like:\\n\\nIf the bug does not reproduce the first time, you have to go back to gathering the information: perhaps an important step has been missed (a particular order of actions, for example) or a state of the data (the user already had an avatar uploaded).\\n\\nWhere and how it is used\\nThis approach is used at every stage of a bug\'s life cycle: at the initial acceptance of the bug report, during the developer\'s analysis, when writing an automated test for that scenario and during the regression testing after the fix. Well-described steps to reproduce are the key to a quick, quality fix.\\n\\nConclusion: the ability to reproduce a bug is critically important for effective debugging. It turns a chaotic problem into a clear algorithm, which can be analyzed, fixed and covered by tests, preventing the error from appearing again in the future.","// 1. The steps received from the user:\\n// \\"On the profile page, after changing the avatar twice, when I try to save the settings I get a 500 error.\\"\\n\\n// 2. The refined steps to reproduce:\\n//   a) Log in as the test user (ID: 123).\\n//   b) Go to the page /profile/edit.\\n//   c) Upload a new avatar file twice (the \\"Upload\\" button).\\n//   d) Change any text field (\\"Name\\", for example).\\n//   e) Press the \\"Save changes\\" button.\\n//   The expected result: the settings are saved.\\n//   The actual result: a response from the server with code 500.\\n\\n// 3. Reproducing it in a controlled environment (by means of a script, for example):\\n// We use the same backend, the same version of the code and the same user data.\\n// We repeat the steps by hand or automate them.\\n// We record the result and the logs (the network requests, the errors in the console, the server logs)."],[2922,"Development through feature branches is a popular Git strategy in which a separate branch is created off the main one (off main or develop, for example) for every new task (a feature, a bugfix). The developer works in their own isolated branch without touching the stable code. Once the work has finished and been tested, the branch is merged back into the main one through a Pull Request or a Merge Request. That lets the team work in parallel, isolate changes and carry out code review before integrating.","Development through feature branches (the Feature Branch Workflow) is an approach to version management that uses Git in order to isolate the work on individual tasks. The basic idea is that all of the development of new functionality is carried out not in the main branch but in a branch created specially for that purpose. That creates a safe environment for experiments and changes without threatening the stability of the main code.\\n\\nThe main steps of the process\\n\\n- Creating the branch: a new branch is created off the stable main branch (off `main` or `develop`, for example). The branch\'s name normally reflects the task — `feature/add-user-login` or `fix/header-overflow`, for example.\\n- Development in isolation: every commit related to that task is made inside the feature branch that has been created. The developer can make changes freely without worrying about conflicts with other people\'s work.\\n- Integration through a Pull/Merge Request: once the work has finished, the branch is not merged directly. Instead a Pull Request (GitHub/GitLab) or a Merge Request is created. That starts the process of discussion, automated testing (CI) and mandatory code review by the other members of the team.\\n- Merging: once the PR has been approved, the feature branch is merged into the target branch (into `develop`, for example). A squash merge or rebase strategy is often used in order to keep the history clean.\\n- Deleting the branch: after a successful merge the feature branch is normally deleted, so as not to clutter the list of branches in the repository.\\n\\nA practical example of the commands\\nHere is the typical sequence of Git commands for working with a feature branch:\\n\\nWhere and why it is used\\nThis approach is the foundation of modern CI/CD practices and is used in practically any team that uses Git. It is especially useful:\\n\\n- In teams of several developers, for working in parallel.\\n- For carrying out mandatory code review, which raises the quality of the code.\\n- For integration with continuous-integration (CI) systems, which run the tests for every feature branch before the merge.\\n- For tracking the history of the changes related to a particular task clearly.\\n\\nConclusion: development through feature branches is a mandatory practice for teamwork, which ensures the isolation of changes, controlled integration and a high quality of code by way of the mandatory review. It is worth applying in any project where more than one person works on the code.","# 1. Switch to the main branch and get the latest changes\\ngit checkout main\\ngit pull origin main\\n\\n# 2. Create and switch to a new branch for the task\\ngit checkout -b feature/add-search-filter\\n\\n# 3. Carry out the work: editing files, commits\\ngit add .\\ngit commit -m \\"Add basic search filter component\\"\\ngit commit -m \\"Implement search logic and styling\\"\\n\\n# 4. Push the branch to the remote repository\\ngit push -u origin feature/add-search-filter\\n\\n# 5. Next a Pull Request is created through GitHub\'s/GitLab\'s web interface.\\n# 6. Once the PR has been approved and merged, the branch can be deleted locally and remotely.\\ngit checkout main\\ngit pull origin main\\ngit branch -d feature/add-search-filter\\ngit push origin --delete feature/add-search-filter"],[2923,"Start by reproducing the bug exactly. Recreate the conditions under which it arises: the environment, the data, the sequence of actions. Then gather the information: check the error logs, the messages in the browser\'s or the terminal\'s console. Isolate the problem, simplifying the code or the data in order to find a minimal reproducible example. Use a debugger in order to step through the code and check the state of the variables. A methodical approach saves time and helps you find the root cause.","Looking for a bug is a systematic process of investigation, not random guesswork. The first and most important step is to understand exactly what is not working and under what conditions.\\n\\n1. Reproduction and localization\\nTry to reproduce the error consistently. If the bug is sporadic, gather as much information as you can about when it shows itself. Determine the boundaries of the problem: does it arise only in a particular browser, with particular input data, or after a specific sequence of actions has been performed? Creating a Minimal Reproducible Example (MRE) often leads to an understanding of the root cause in itself.\\n\\n2. Gathering the information and analysis\\nUse every available source of data:\\n\\n- The application\'s and the server\'s logs: look for stack traces, error messages, warnings.\\n- The browser\'s developer tools (for the web): the Console, the Network tab for checking the requests/responses, the debugger (Sources/Debugger).\\n- The system logs and monitoring: if the problem is related to memory, the processor or the network.\\n\\nAnalyze the changes: did the bug appear after a recent update of the code, of the libraries or of the configuration? Use `git bisect` to look for the problem commit.\\n\\n3. Debugging methods\\nOnce the problem has been localized to a module or a function, use active debugging:\\n\\n- A debugger: set breakpoints and execute the code step by step (step into/over), watching the values of the variables.\\n- Logging: add logs at the key points, in order to track the flow of execution and the state of the data. For complex asynchronous operations that is especially useful.\\n- Rubber-duck debugging: try explaining the code and the problem in detail to a colleague or even to an inanimate object. The solution is often found in the course of the explanation.\\n\\n4. An example of the approach (JavaScript)\\n\\nIn this example the logging will immediately show that the second element has its price in the form of a string, which leads to an incorrect addition. Next you can add validation or a type conversion.\\n\\nConclusion: a systematic approach to looking for bugs — from reproduction and gathering the data to targeted debugging — makes the process predictable and effective. It is especially useful in teamwork, when analysing legacy code or complex distributed systems, where intuition is not enough.","// Suppose the calculateTotal function sometimes returns NaN\\nfunction calculateTotal(items) {\\n    // 1. We log the input data\\n    console.log(\'Items received:\', items);\\n\\n    let total = 0;\\n    for (let item of items) {\\n        // 2. We check every iteration\\n        console.log(\'Processing item:\', item, \'Price:\', item.price);\\n        // 3. We suspect price may be a string or undefined\\n        total += item.price; // A possible source of NaN, if price is not a number\\n    }\\n    // 4. We use the debugger here, in order to check the value of total\\n    debugger;\\n    return total;\\n}\\n\\n// 5. We create a minimal test case in order to reproduce it\\nconst testItems = [\\n    { name: \'A\', price: 10 },\\n    { name: \'B\', price: \'20\' }, // The error: a string!\\n    { name: \'C\', price: 30 }\\n];\\nconsole.log(\'Result:\', calculateTotal(testItems)); // Will print NaN"],[2924,"A pre-release branch is a special branch in Git into which all of the features and fixes ready for release are merged before the final release. It serves as a stabilization ground for the final testing, the building of the artifacts and the verification of the integration. Using such a branch lets you isolate the process of preparing the release from the active development in main/develop, minimizing the risk of unstable code reaching production. It is standard practice in Git Flow and similar models.","A pre-release branch (often called release or release/*) is a key element in branching strategies such as Git Flow. Its main purpose is to create an isolated space for preparing a particular version of the product for release, separating that process from the ongoing development of new functionality.\\n\\nHow it works\\nWhen the team decides that the functionality that has built up in the development branch (in develop, for example) is ready for release, a new branch is created off develop, normally with the name release/v1.2.0. From that moment on:\\n\\n- Only fixes for bugs found during the testing of the release candidate are made in that branch.\\n- The development of new functionality continues in develop and does not reach the release branch.\\n- Extended tests, the building of the final artifacts (docker images, installation packages) and the preparation of the documentation are run on the basis of that branch.\\n\\nPractical application and an example\\nIn a CI/CD pipeline you can configure an automatic build and deployment to the staging environment specifically for branches beginning with release/. That lets the QA team and the product managers test exactly the version that is about to be released.\\n\\nSuch an approach guarantees that the tag in main corresponds to the artifact that was built and tested, and that all of the fixes made during the preparation of the release come back into the main development branch (develop).\\n\\nIn summary: a pre-release branch is a tool for quality control and for stabilizing a version before release. It is worth applying in projects with regular releases, where a clear separation of the development, testing and delivery stages matters.","# An example of creating and working with a pre-release branch in Git\\n# 1. We create the release branch off develop\\ngit checkout develop\\ngit pull origin develop\\ngit checkout -b release/v1.5.0\\n\\n# 2. We make the fixes found during the testing (bugfixes only)\\ngit commit -m \\"fix: resolve issue with login timeout\\"\\n\\n# 3. When the release is ready, we merge the branch into main (production) and back into develop\\ngit checkout main\\ngit merge --no-ff release/v1.5.0\\ngit tag -a v1.5.0 -m \\"Release version 1.5.0\\"\\n\\ngit checkout develop\\ngit merge --no-ff release/v1.5.0\\n\\n# 4. We delete the temporary release branch\\ngit branch -d release/v1.5.0"],[2925,"Rules for the formatting of commit messages help you create an intelligible history of the changes. The Conventional Commits approach is often used, where the message begins with a type (feat, fix, docs and so on) and is then followed by a brief description. The message should be in the imperative mood — \\"Add user login feature\\", for example. The first line is the subject (up to 50 characters), then a blank line and a detailed description. That makes the automatic generation of a changelog, and the understanding of the changes, easier.","Rules for the formatting of commit messages are the conventions teams use for uniformity and clarity in the history of changes in a version-control system such as Git. Well-written messages help you understand quickly what was changed and why, without having to study every file. That matters especially when working together, when analysing the history in order to roll a change back, or when generating changelogs automatically.\\n\\nThe main principles and formats\\nThe most popular standard today is Conventional Commits. It proposes a simple, machine-readable format:\\n\\n- The type: indicates the nature of the change. The main types: feat (new functionality), fix (an error fix), docs (changes to the documentation), style (formatting, no changes to the code), refactor (refactoring the code), test (adding or fixing tests), chore (updating the dependencies, configuring the build).\\n- The scope: an optional element, which specifies the part of the project — (auth), (ui), (api), for example.\\n- The description: a brief description of the change in the imperative mood — \\"add\\", \\"fix\\", \\"update\\", rather than \\"added\\", \\"fixed\\", for example.\\n\\nThe structure of a message\\n\\nAn example of a good commit message:\\n\\nIn this example the first line is the subject, then a blank line and the body with a bulleted list of the details. The footer can carry a link to the task in the tracker (Closes #123).\\n\\nPractical advice\\n\\n- The subject: should be short (preferably up to 50 characters) and clear.\\n- The body: is used to explain what was changed and why, rather than how (that can be seen in the diff). Describe the context and the consequences of the change.\\n- The language: English is normally used, but that depends on the team.\\n- The tools: to check the rules and enforce them you can use Git hooks (the commit-msg hook) or linters such as commitlint.\\n\\nConclusion: following the rules for the formatting of commit messages (the Conventional Commits format especially) is critically important for keeping a project\'s history clean, for automating processes (semantic versioning, for example) and for effective communication within the team. It is worth applying in any project where more than one person works on the code or where the traceability of the changes matters.","<type>(<scope>): <description>\\n\\n[optional body]\\n\\n[optional footer(s)]\\n\\nfeat(auth): add JWT token validation middleware\\n\\n- Implement middleware to verify JWT in Authorization header\\n- Add error handling for invalid or expired tokens\\n- Update API documentation for protected endpoints\\n\\nCloses #123"],[2926,"Pre-commit hooks are scripts that Git runs automatically before a commit is created. They are used to check code — to run linters, formatters or tests, for example. You can set them up by hand, by creating files in .git/hooks/, or by means of tools such as Husky for convenience. That helps prevent code with errors, or code that does not conform to the style, from reaching the repository.","Pre-commit hooks are a Git mechanism that lets you perform certain actions (scripts) automatically immediately before changes are recorded in the repository. Their main purpose is to ensure code-quality control by running checks such as linting, formatting or the running of unit tests before the changes are committed. That prevents code with obvious errors, or code that violates the team\'s conventions, from reaching the project\'s history.\\n\\nHow to set it up by hand\\nIn every Git repository there is a hidden `.git/hooks/` folder, containing example scripts for various events. To create a pre-commit hook you have to:\\n- Go into the `.git/hooks/` folder.\\n- Create the file `pre-commit`, or rename `pre-commit.sample` to `pre-commit` (removing the `.sample` extension).\\n- Make the file executable (`chmod +x pre-commit` on Unix systems).\\n- Write a script inside it, in Bash, Python, Node.js or another language, which will perform the checks you need. If the script finishes with a non-zero return code, the commit will be cancelled.\\n\\nUsing tools (Husky and lint-staged)\\nSetting it up by hand is inconvenient for teamwork, since the hooks are not copied when the repository is cloned. That is why the combination of Husky and lint-staged is popular in modern JavaScript/Node.js projects.\\n- Husky makes managing Git hooks easier, letting you configure them through the project\'s configuration file (through `package.json`, for example).\\n- lint-staged runs certain commands (ESLint or Prettier, for example) only for the files added to the staging area (the index), which speeds the checks up.\\n\\nAn example of the setup in a Node.js project:\\n\\nOnce the dependencies have been installed, initialize Husky with the command `npm run prepare`, then create the pre-commit hook that will run lint-staged:\\n\\nNow, on every attempt to commit, ESLint and Prettier will be run automatically for all of the JavaScript files that have changed. If the checks do not pass, the commit will be cancelled.\\n\\nWhere it is used\\nPre-commit hooks are widely used in team development for:\\n- Checking the syntax and the style of the code (ESLint, Pylint, RuboCop).\\n- Formatting automatically (Prettier, Black).\\n- Running unit tests for the files that have changed.\\n- Checking for the presence of secrets (keys, passwords) in the code.\\n- Validating commit messages (by means of commitlint, for example).\\n\\nConclusion: pre-commit hooks are an effective way of automating routine checks and maintaining a high quality of codebase. They are worth applying in any project, especially when working in a team, in order to minimize human error and ensure a single standard of code.","#!/bin/bash\\n# An example of a simple pre-commit hook, checking for the presence of console.log in JavaScript files\\necho \\"Running pre-commit checks...\\"\\nif git diff --cached --name-only | xargs grep -l \\"console\\\\.log\\"; then\\n    echo \\"Error: Found console.log statements in staged files.\\"\\n    exit 1\\nfi\\necho \\"Checks passed.\\"\\n\\n// package.json\\n{\\n  \\"scripts\\": {\\n    \\"prepare\\": \\"husky install\\"\\n  },\\n  \\"devDependencies\\": {\\n    \\"husky\\": \\"^8.0.0\\",\\n    \\"lint-staged\\": \\"^13.0.0\\",\\n    \\"eslint\\": \\"^8.0.0\\",\\n    \\"prettier\\": \\"^2.0.0\\"\\n  },\\n  \\"lint-staged\\": {\\n    \\"*.js\\": [\\"eslint --fix\\", \\"prettier --write\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nnpx husky add .husky/pre-commit \\"npx lint-staged\\""],[2927,"The squashing of commits is an operation in Git that combines several consecutive commits into one new commit. It is done in order to simplify the history of the changes, especially before a branch is merged into the main one. When working on a feature, for example, a developer may make a lot of small commits that logically represent one task. Squashing lets you present that work as a single whole, which makes the project\'s history more readable and more intelligible.","The squashing of commits is a technique in the Git version-control system intended for cleaning a project\'s history up. It lets you combine several commits into one, preserving all of the changes but presenting them as a single logical block of work.\\n\\nWhat is squashing for?\\nIn the course of development programmers often create a multitude of small commits: \\"fixed a typo\\", \\"added a check\\", \\"refactored\\". For local work that is convenient, but when merging into the main branch such detail clutters the history, making it harder to read and to analyse. Squashing solves that problem by grouping all of the changes related to one task or feature into one meaningful commit with an intelligible message.\\n\\nHow is squashing performed?\\nMost often squashing is performed during a `git rebase` (in interactive mode) or a `git merge` (with the `--squash` option).\\n\\n- Through an interactive rebase: that gives you full control over the history. You specify which commits are to be collapsed.\\n- Through a merge with squash: when a branch is merged into the main one, all of the commits from the branch are combined into one new commit in the target branch.\\n\\nA practical example\\nSuppose you have three commits in the branch `feature/login`:\\n\\nYou want to combine them into one commit before merging into `main`. Let us use an interactive rebase:\\n\\nIn the editor that opens, for the first two commits we change `pick` to `squash` or `s`, leaving `pick` for the last one (or the first, depending on the order). Once it has been saved, Git will offer to create a new, single commit message.\\n\\nWhere and when to apply it?\\nSquashing is especially useful in working processes based on Pull/Merge Requests (in GitFlow or GitHub Flow, for example). All of the commits from the feature branch are combined into one at the merge, which makes the history of the `main` branch linear and clean. You should not, however, apply squashing to commits that have already been published to a shared repository, so as not to rewrite the shared history.\\n\\nIn summary: the squashing of commits is a tool for maintaining the cleanliness and the readability of a Git history. It is worth applying before feature branches are merged into the main one, in order to present finished work as a single whole, making the navigation and the analysis of the project\'s history easier.","git log --oneline\\n# a1b2c3d Added email validation\\n# e4f5g6h Fixed an error in the condition\\n# i7j8k9l Updated the button\'s text\\n\\ngit rebase -i HEAD~3"],[2928,"A branch is an independent line of development in Git. It lets you work on new functionality or on fixes without touching the project\'s main version. Once the work has finished, the changes from the branch can be combined with the main branch.","A branch is a kind of \\"copy\\" of a project at a particular moment in time, in which you can make changes without affecting the main version of the code. The main branch is often called `main`, for example, and other branches can be created off it in order to develop new functionality.\\n\\nThe commands for working with branches:\\n- Create a branch:\\n\\n- Switch to a branch:\\n\\n- Create one and switch to it at once:\\n\\nThe advantages of branching:\\n- Parallel work: every developer can work in their own branch.\\n- Safety: the changes that have been made do not touch the main branch until they are ready.\\n- A history of the changes: Git saves every action in the branch, letting you go back if need be.\\n\\nOnce work on a branch has finished, the changes are combined with the main branch by means of the `git merge` command. That helps you maintain the project\'s structure and avoid errors.\\n\\nAn example: you develop new functionality in the branch `feature/login`, test it, and then merge it with `main`. That makes development flexible and safe.","git branch feature/new-feature\\n\\ngit checkout feature/new-feature\\n\\ngit checkout -b feature/new-feature"],[2929,"The Centralized Workflow. The default development branch is main, and every change is recorded in that branch. This working process requires no branches other than the main one.\\n\\nThe Feature Branching Workflow. The main idea of the feature-branching working process is that the development of all of the functionality should be carried out in a special branch rather than in the main one.\\n\\nThe Gitflow Workflow. Gitflow defines a strict branching model designed around a project\'s release. That provides a reliable foundation for managing large projects. It assigns very specific roles to the various branches and defines how and when they are to interact. In addition to the feature branches, separate branches are used for the preparation, the maintenance and the recording of releases.\\n\\nThe Forking Workflow. The Forking Workflow differs fundamentally from the other popular Git working processes. Instead of using a single server-side repository as the “central” codebase, it gives every developer their own server-side repository. That means every participant has not one but two Git repositories: a private local one and a public server-side one. The Forking Workflow is most often met with in public open-source projects.","The Centralized Workflow. The default development branch is main, and every change is recorded in that branch. This working process requires no branches other than the main one.\\n\\nThe Feature Branching Workflow. The main idea of the feature-branching working process is that the development of all of the functionality should be carried out in a special branch rather than in the main one.\\n\\nThe Gitflow Workflow. Gitflow defines a strict branching model designed around a project\'s release. That provides a reliable foundation for managing large projects. It assigns very specific roles to the various branches and defines how and when they are to interact. In addition to the feature branches, separate branches are used for the preparation, the maintenance and the recording of releases.\\n\\nThe Forking Workflow. The Forking Workflow differs fundamentally from the other popular Git working processes. Instead of using a single server-side repository as the “central” codebase, it gives every developer their own server-side repository. That means every participant has not one but two Git repositories: a private local one and a public server-side one. The Forking Workflow is most often met with in public open-source projects.",null],[2930,"The `.gitignore` file defines the files and folders Git is to ignore. It is used in order to exclude temporary files, logs, dependencies, generated files and other unnecessary items.","When you work on a project, it may contain files that do not need to be added to the repository — for example:\\n- Temporary files (`.tmp`, `.log`).\\n- Generated folders (`node_modules`, `dist`).\\n- Confidential data (API keys, passwords).\\n\\nPatterns for such files and folders are written into the `.gitignore` file. An example:\\n\\nGit will ignore the files specified, but only if they have not already been added to the repository. If a file has already been recorded, it has to be removed from the index:\\n\\n`.gitignore` makes the work easier and prevents unnecessary files from being added to the repository by accident.","# We exclude node_modules\\nnode_modules/\\n\\n# We ignore the editor\'s temporary files\\n*.tmp\\n*.swp\\n\\n# We ignore generated files\\ndist/\\n\\ngit rm --cached <file_name>"],[2931,"Gitflow is a branching strategy that divides development into several branches: `main` for the releases, `develop` for the active development, and additional branches for new functionality, bug fixes and releases. This process is convenient for large projects with clear development stages.","The Gitflow working process structures the work with branches in order to make it orderly and intelligible. The main components:\\n- The `main` branch:\\n\\nContains stable code, ready for release.\\n- The `develop` branch:\\n\\nUsed for the active development. All of the new functionality is added here.\\n- Feature branches:\\n\\nCreated off `develop` in order to work on new functionality.\\n- Once the work has finished, they are merged back into `develop`.\\n\\n- Release branches:\\n\\nCreated in order to prepare a release.\\n\\nBugs are fixed in them and documentation is added. Once finished, the work is merged into `main` and `develop`.\\n- Hotfix branches:\\n\\nCreated off `main` in order to fix errors in production quickly.\\n\\nOnce the fix has been made, the branch is merged into `main` and `develop`.\\n\\nThe advantages of Gitflow:\\n- A separation of the development and the releases.\\n- Convenient management of functionality, bugs and releases.\\n\\nAn example: if you are putting out an update with new functionality, the process might look like this:\\n- The development of the functionality in `feature` branches.\\n- A merge with `develop` once it has finished.\\n- The creation of a `release` branch in order to prepare the release.\\n- After the release, the fixing of bugs in `hotfix` branches.\\n\\nGitflow suits complex projects well, but it can be excessive for small teams.","git checkout -b feature/awesome-feature develop\\n\\ngit checkout -b hotfix/critical-bug main"],[2932,"Git lets you track changes in your code, work in a team without the risk of losing data, create branches for new functionality and then combine them easily. It is a fast, reliable and popular solution for version management.","Git provides a multitude of advantages, including:\\n- Tracking the changes: you can see who made a change and when, compare versions and go back to the project\'s previous states.\\n- Being distributed: every developer has a complete copy of the repository, which ensures reliability — the data is not lost even if the server fails.\\n- Branching and merging: Git lets you create independent branches for new functionality or fixes and then merge them with the main branch.\\n- Working in a team: Git synchronizes the changes between the project\'s participants, preventing conflicts and the overwriting of data.\\n- Speed: local operations such as recording a change happen very fast, because Git works locally.\\n- Integration with tools: Git integrates easily with platforms such as GitHub, GitLab and Bitbucket, for the convenient management of repositories and for working together.\\n\\nGit helps developers manage their code effectively, speeds teamwork up and makes the development process more reliable.",null],[2933,"A distributed version-control system (Git, for example) saves a complete copy of the repository with every participant in the project. That makes the work more reliable, lets you work offline and makes merging changes easy.","In distributed version-control systems (DVCS) every developer has a local copy of the complete repository, including the whole history of the changes.\\n\\nExamples of such systems: Git, Mercurial.\\n\\nThe advantages of distributed systems:\\n- Independence from a central server: every operation, such as recording a change or comparing versions, is performed locally, and the server is used only for synchronization.\\n- Working offline: you can work on the project without a connection to the internet.\\n- Backup: if the server breaks down, the repository can be restored from any local computer.\\n- Simplified work with branches: local branches are easy to create and to combine without affecting the other developers.\\n\\nThe difference from centralized systems (from Subversion, for example):\\n- In centralized systems all of the data is held on a single server, which makes it vulnerable to failures and requires a constant connection.\\n\\nA distributed system makes development more flexible and more reliable.",null],[2934,"Git is written in the C programming language. That choice ensures high performance and efficiency.","Git was developed by Linus Torvalds in 2005, and he chose the C language in which to write the system. The main reason is the high speed of its operation and the low level of resources needed to perform the operations.\\n\\nWhy the C language was chosen:\\n- Performance: operations such as comparing changes, merging branches and working with the history are performed fast.\\n- Control over the memory: C lets you optimize the use of memory, which matters for working with large repositories.\\n- Portability: programs in C can be run on most operating systems, which makes Git a universal tool.\\n\\nInterfaces for working with Git, such as GitHub, may use other languages (JavaScript or Ruby, for example), but Git\'s own foundation remains in the C language.",null],[2935,"A Git repository is a store in which every change to your project is tracked, including the files, their history and the branches. To initialize a repository, use the `git init` command, which creates a hidden `.git` folder in which Git saves its data.","A Git repository is a data structure used to hold the whole history of a project\'s changes. With it you can track, save and restore changes, and work in a team.\\n\\nInitializing a repository:\\n\\nCreate a folder or go into an existing one by means of `cd`:\\n\\nRun the `git init` command:\\n\\nThat will create a hidden `.git` folder, in which Git holds all of its data.\\n\\nOnce it has been initialized, you can add files, record changes (`git add`, `git commit`) and link the repository with a remote server (`git remote add`).\\n\\nAn example:","cd my-project\\n\\ngit init\\n\\nmkdir my-project\\ncd my-project\\ngit init\\ntouch README.md\\ngit add README.md\\ngit commit -m \\"Initial commit\\""],[2936,"The `git clone` command creates a copy of an existing repository, including the whole history of the changes, the files and the branches. It is used in order to start work on a project that is already in a remote repository.","When you use `git clone`, Git copies the whole repository onto your local computer. That includes:\\n- All of the project\'s files: their current state.\\n- The history of the changes: every commit, branch and tag.\\n\\nAn example of its use:\\n\\nOnce the command has run, Git:\\n- Creates the folder `project` (or the name given).\\n- Downloads the repository\'s contents into it.\\n- Sets up the link with the remote repository (`origin`).\\n\\nAdditional parameters let you configure the branch, the depth of the copy or the folder\'s name:\\n\\nThis command is convenient for obtaining a complete copy of a project with minimal configuration.","git clone https://github.com/username/project.git\\n\\ngit clone -b develop --depth 1 https://github.com/username/project.git custom-folder"],[2937,"HEAD in Git is a pointer to the current branch or commit. It shows what you are working on at the moment and changes when you switch branches or make commits.","HEAD is a special pointer in Git, which points at the \\"active\\" branch or commit. When you make changes or run commands, Git uses HEAD in order to determine where to save the new data.\\n\\nScenarios for working with HEAD:\\n\\nThe ordinary state: HEAD points at the latest recorded change of the current branch. In the `main` branch, for example, HEAD will point at its latest commit.\\n\\n\\"Detached HEAD\\":\\n- If you switch to a particular commit rather than to a branch, HEAD will point at that commit, but the changes will not be saved in a branch.\\n\\n- To go back to a branch:\\n\\n- An example of a command for checking:\\n\\nHEAD is normally marked as `HEAD -> `.\\n\\nHEAD is an important navigation tool, which lets Git understand what you are working on at any given moment.","git checkout <commit_hash>\\n\\ngit checkout main\\n\\ngit log --oneline"],[2938,"The `git stash` command saves your current unfinished changes in a special place (a stack), so that you can switch to another branch or perform other actions. Those changes can be restored later by means of `git stash apply` or `git stash pop`.","Sometimes you need to set your current work aside temporarily in order to switch to another task, but the changes are not ready to be committed yet. In such cases `git stash` helps.\\n\\nThe main actions:\\n- Saving the changes:\\n\\nThat removes the changes from the working directory and the staging area, saving them in the stack.\\n- Viewing the changes that have been saved:\\n\\nRestoring the changes:\\n- Applying the changes, leaving them in the stack:\\n\\n- Applying them and removing them from the stack:\\n\\n- Removing them from the stack:\\n\\nAn example: you are working in the `main` branch and want to switch to the `feature` branch, but do not want to lose your current changes:\\n\\nThe `git stash` command helps you work flexibly while preserving your progress.","git stash\\n\\ngit stash list\\n\\ngit stash apply\\n\\ngit stash pop\\n\\ngit stash drop\\n\\ngit stash\\ngit checkout feature\\ngit stash apply"],[2939,"To delete a local branch:\\n\\nIf the branch contains unfinished changes:\\n\\nTo delete a remote branch:","Deleting branches helps you maintain order in a project, especially once the work on a task has finished.\\n\\nDeleting a local branch:\\n\\nDeleting a branch whose changes have already been merged:\\n\\nDeleting it forcibly, if the branch has not been merged:\\n\\nDeleting a remote branch: for that the following command is used:\\n\\nAn example of the complete process: you have finished the work on a `feature` branch, merged it into `develop` and want to delete it:\\n\\nIt matters to make sure the work in the branch has finished before you delete it.","git branch -d <branch_name>\\n\\ngit branch -D <branch_name>\\n\\ngit push origin --delete <branch_name>\\n\\ngit checkout develop\\ngit branch -d feature\\ngit push origin --delete feature"],[2940,"`git reflog` shows the history of every movement of HEAD, including commits, merges and branch switches. This command is useful for restoring lost commits or branches.","Git keeps a journal of every change of HEAD — that is precisely the `reflog`. Even if you have deleted a branch or a commit, it stays in the `reflog` for 30 days (by default).\\n\\nThe command:\\n\\nIt shows a list of every operation, including their hashes — for example:\\n\\nApplying the `reflog`:\\n\\nRestoring a lost commit: if you have deleted a branch or reset a commit by accident:\\n\\nViewing the operations in the repository: you can see what actions have been performed, even if they are not in `git log`.\\n\\nAn example: if you have reset the `feature` branch but want to restore it:\\n\\n`git reflog` is a rescue tool for working with the history of changes.","git reflog\\n\\na1b2c3d HEAD@{0}: commit: Updated README\\nd4e5f6g HEAD@{1}: checkout: moving from feature to main\\n\\ngit reset --hard <commit_hash>\\n\\ngit reflog\\ngit checkout -b feature <commit_hash>"],[2941,"To undo a commit that has been pushed to the server, you can use the `git revert` command, which will create a new commit undoing the changes. It matters that this is a safe method, since the history of the changes is preserved.","When a commit has already been pushed to a remote repository (by means of `git push`, for example), it cannot be deleted without the risk of damaging the history, especially if other developers may already have downloaded it. The `git revert` command is used instead, which creates a new commit undoing the changes.\\n\\nUsing `git revert`: the command `git revert <commit_hash>` creates a new commit that inverts the changes made in the commit specified. An example:\\n\\nThat is safe, because only the result of the commit is undone, while the history itself is preserved.\\n\\nUsing `git reset` for local changes (not recommended for public commits): if the commit has not been pushed to the server yet, you can use `git reset`. But if the commit has already been pushed and published, it is better to use `git revert`, so as to avoid conflicts with the other participants in the project.\\n\\nWhy you should not use `git reset` for public commits: deleting commits (by means of `git reset` or `git push --force`) on data that has already been made public can create problems for the other developers, since they may run into an inconsistency of the history.","git revert abc1234\\ngit push origin main"],[2942,"If a branch has been deleted locally, it can be restored by using the `git reflog` command. If a branch has been deleted remotely, it can be restored by means of `git push origin <branch_name>`.","Restoring a local branch: if a branch has been deleted locally, you can restore it by means of `git reflog`, which holds the history of every movement of HEAD. For that you have to find the commit at which the branch was active and create a new branch:\\n\\nRestoring a remote branch (if it has been deleted from the server): if a branch has been deleted from the remote repository, it can be restored by pushing it back to the server:\\n\\nA note: if you work in a team, always check who else may depend on that branch before restoring it.","git reflog\\ngit checkout -b <branch_name> <commit_hash>\\n\\ngit checkout -b <branch_name> <commit_hash>\\ngit push origin <branch_name>"],[2943,"When a conflict arises in Git, you have to resolve the divergences in the files by hand and then finish the merge by means of the `git merge --continue` command.","Merge conflicts arise when Git cannot combine the changes in two branches automatically, because the changes in one and the same section of code are incompatible. Git marks such sections in the files with special markers, and you have to choose by hand which changes to keep.\\n\\nThe steps for resolving a conflict:\\n- Switch to the branch into which you want to merge the changes:\\n\\n- Run the merge command:\\n\\n- Resolve the conflicts by hand: Git will mark the conflicts in the files. Open those files in order to see the conflicting changes. You will see markers like these:\\n\\nRemove the markers and choose which changes to keep.\\n- Add the corrected files to the index: once you have resolved every conflict, add the files to the index:\\n\\n- Finish the merge: when every conflict has been resolved, finish the merge by means of:\\n\\n- Finish the merge with a commit: Git will perform the merge commit, and you can push the changes to the remote repository:\\n\\nConflicts are a normal part of working with Git, and resolving them properly lets you preserve the project\'s integrity.","git checkout main\\n\\ngit merge feature-branch\\n\\n<<<<<<< HEAD\\n// Your version\\n=======\\n// The version from the feature-branch branch\\n>>>>>>> feature-branch\\n\\ngit add <file_name>\\n\\ngit merge --continue\\n\\ngit push origin main"],[2944,"The `git cherry-pick` command lets you select an individual commit from another branch and apply it to your current branch, bypassing the other changes that were made in that same branch. That is useful when you need to carry just one commit across rather than the whole set of changes.","`git cherry-pick` lets you extract individual commits from other branches and apply them. That is useful if you want to carry particular changes from one branch into another without merging the whole branch. You want to carry an error fix from the `feature-branch` branch into the main `main` branch, for example.\\n\\nAn example of its use:\\n\\nSwitch to the branch into which you want to carry the commit:\\n\\nUse `git cherry-pick` with the hash of the commit you want to carry across:\\n\\nGit applies the changes from the commit `abc1234` to your current branch and creates a new commit.\\n\\nIf a conflict arises while the `cherry-pick` is being performed, you will have to resolve it by hand and finish the merge process, just as in the case of an ordinary merge.","git checkout main\\n\\ngit cherry-pick abc1234"],[2945,"To look for the commit that broke the code after a merge, you can use the `git bisect` command. This tool helps you find the commit causing the problem by way of a binary search.","After a merge with a branch, problems may arise and you have to work out which commit caused the error. The `git bisect` tool helps with that, performing a binary search through the history of the commits and gradually narrowing the range of possible culprits.\\n\\nThe steps for using `git bisect`:\\n- Initialize the binary search:\\n\\n- Specify a good commit (where everything worked) and a bad commit (where the error arose):\\n\\n- Git will switch to the commits between the good and the bad state one at a time. After every switch you have to check whether the error arises.\\n- Once the problem commit has been found:\\n\\nAn example:\\n- First find a working state:\\n\\n- Then find the commit with the error:\\n\\nGit will carry the search out automatically and report which commit caused the problem.","git bisect start\\n\\ngit bisect good <good_commit_hash>\\ngit bisect bad <bad_commit_hash>\\n\\ngit bisect reset\\n\\ngit bisect good abc1234\\n\\ngit bisect bad def5678"],[2946,"`git reset --mixed`: undoes the changes that were added to the index but preserves the changes in the working directory.\\n\\n`git merge --abort`: cancels the merge that is currently in progress and returns the repository to the state it was in before the merge began.","`git reset --mixed`: this command undoes the changes that were added to the index by means of `git add`, but leaves the changes in the working directory (the changes you have made in the files are preserved). That is useful when you want to undo the addition of files to the index without losing the changes themselves.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThat removes the files from the index but not from the working directory.\\n\\n`git merge --abort`:\\n\\nWhen you perform a merge and a conflict arises, you can use `git merge --abort` in order to cancel the merge process and return to the state before the merge began. That is useful if you do not want to resolve the conflicts and want to stop the merge.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThat returns your repository to the state before the merge began, including the undoing of every change in the working directory related to that merge.\\n\\nSo `git reset --mixed` is useful for working with the index, while `git merge --abort` is for interrupting a merge.","git reset --mixed\\n\\ngit merge --abort"],[2947,"To combine the last N commits into one, the command `git rebase -i HEAD~N` is used, which lets you edit the commits interactively. In the course of it you can choose which commits to combine by means of the `squash` or `fixup` command.","Combining several commits into one is called the squashing of commits and is performed by means of an interactive rebase. The process looks as follows:\\n\\nOpen an interactive rebase:\\n\\nwhere `N` is the number of the last commits you want to combine. To combine the last 3 commits, for example, use `HEAD~3`.\\n\\nIn the editor you will see a list of the commits:\\n\\nChange `pick` to `squash` or `fixup` for every commit except the first — for example:\\n\\nSave and close the editor. Git will combine the commits into one.\\n\\nIf need be, edit the message for the new, combined commit.\\n\\nFinish the rebase:\\n\\nThis process combines the commits selected into one and improves the readability of the history.","git rebase -i HEAD~N\\n\\npick a1b2c3d Commit 1\\npick d4e5f6g Commit 2\\npick h7i8j9k Commit 3\\n\\npick a1b2c3d Commit 1\\nsquash d4e5f6g Commit 2\\nsquash h7i8j9k Commit 3\\n\\ngit rebase --continue"],[2948,"Hooks in Git are scripts that are run automatically on certain actions in a repository, such as commits, merges or pushes. They are located in the `.git/hooks` directory and can be configured to perform various tasks.","Git hooks are scripts that can be called automatically in response to certain actions in a repository. The hooks are located in your repository\'s `.git/hooks` folder. They can be written in various languages (in Bash or Python, for example), but are normally used for automatic checks — for linting, testing or blocking unwanted operations, for example.\\n\\nExamples of hooks:\\n- `pre-commit`: runs before every commit. Can be used to check the code for errors or for formatting.\\n- `post-commit`: runs after a commit has been performed, can be used for notifications or updates.\\n- `pre-push`: runs before a push to a remote repository, used to check the changes before they are sent.\\n- `commit-msg`: runs before the commit message is recorded, normally used to check the message\'s format.\\n\\nAn example of its use:\\n- Go into `.git/hooks`.\\n- Copy the hook\'s template (`pre-commit.sample`, for example) into the file `pre-commit`.\\n- Write the script you want to run at the commit.\\n\\nTo enable a hook it is enough to make it executable:","chmod +x .git/hooks/pre-commit"],[2949,"`git clone` is used in order to create a copy of a remote repository on your local computer, including all of its commits and history.\\n\\n`git remote` manages remote repositories, letting you add, remove or change the remote URLs for your local repository.","`git clone`: the `git clone` command is used in order to copy a remote repository onto your local computer. In doing so, all of the repository\'s files, commits and history are copied. This command is normally used once, in order to create a local copy of a remote repository. An example:\\n\\n`git remote`: the `git remote` command is used in order to manage remote repositories. It lets you add new remote repositories, change their URLs or remove them. If you want to add a remote repository in order to push changes to GitHub, for example, you can use `git remote add`. An example:\\n\\nThe `git remote -v` command lets you view a list of every remote repository linked with the local repository:\\n\\nThe main difference:\\n- `git clone` creates a local copy of a repository.\\n- `git remote` manages the remote repositories linked with the local repository.","git clone https://github.com/user/repository.git\\n\\ngit remote add origin https://github.com/user/repository.git\\n\\ngit remote -v"],[2950,"A pull request (PR) is a request to merge changes from one branch into another, normally with the aim of discussing and reviewing the changes before they are integrated into the main branch. A PR is used when a developer wants to propose their changes to the team for review and discussion.","A pull request (PR) is a request to merge the changes made in one branch into another, normally into the main branch or into the development branch. In a PR you can discuss the changes, review the code and discuss possible improvements or corrections before those changes are merged into the project\'s main branch.\\n\\nWhen to use a PR:\\n- When developing together in a team, in order to review and discuss the changes.\\n- In order to avoid errors, since the changes can be reviewed by the other developers before the merge.\\n- When a code review is required.\\n- In order to maintain the cleanliness of the commit history in the main branch, since the changes go through a review before being integrated.\\n\\nAn example of its use:\\n- The developer creates a new branch in order to work on a feature.\\n- Once the work has finished, they push the changes to the remote repository.\\n- The developer opens a pull request, proposing the changes for merging into the main branch.\\n- The other developers review the code and, if everything is in order, the changes are merged.",null],[2951,"`git revert` is used in order to create a new commit that undoes the changes made in a previous commit. This command is useful when changes have to be rolled back without changing the repository\'s history.","The `git revert` command is used in order to undo the changes made in one or more commits, by way of creating a new commit that in effect undoes the changes of the previous one.\\n\\nUnlike the `git reset` command, which changes the repository\'s history, `git revert` preserves the history and creates a new commit that \\"rolls back\\" the changes added in a particular commit.\\n\\nAn example of its use:\\n\\nTo undo the changes in a particular commit, use its hash:\\n\\nAfter that Git will create a new commit that will undo the changes introduced in the commit specified.\\n\\nThat is useful if you want to undo a commit that has already been pushed to a remote repository, so as not to change the repository\'s history, which could create problems for the other participants in the project.","git revert <commit-hash>"],[2952,"A fork is used when you want to create a copy of someone else\'s repository in order to make changes and propose them back to the owners. A branch is used for working with changes within a single repository, most often within a single project.","A fork is the process of creating a copy of someone else\'s repository, normally in order to propose changes to a project without having access to its main repository. A fork creates an independent copy of the repository, in which you can work. That is normally used in open-source projects, where you do not have write rights to the main repository but want to propose changes.\\n\\nAn example of its use:\\n- Open-source projects: if you want to make changes to a repository to which you have no access rights, you fork it, make the changes in your own copy and send a pull request to the original repository.\\n- Working in a team with divided rights: in the case where a team works on several projects, a fork can be created in order to work on a separate project, so that the other participants can put their own proposals forward.\\n\\nA branch, on the other hand, is created within the same repository and is used for working on various features or tasks in one project, without creating a separate copy of the repository.\\n\\nWhen to use which:\\n- A fork — for working with other people\'s repositories, when changes have to be made to a project to which you have no write rights.\\n- A branch — for working inside your own project or repository, when changes have to be isolated by functionality or by task, but within the same repository.",null],[2953,"`git rebase` moves one branch on top of another, copying the commits and rewriting their base. It takes the set of commits starting from the point of divergence and applies them to the new base one after another, creating new commits with the same changes but a different parent. That lets you obtain a linear history without merge commits.","The mechanism of its operation:\\n- Git finds the common ancestor (the `fork point`) of the branch and the target base.\\n- \\"Detaches\\" the branch\'s commits, forming a batch of patches.\\n- Applies the patches to the target branch one at a time, creating new SHAs.\\n\\nThe commands:\\n- `git checkout feature && git rebase main`\\n- Resolving the conflicts at the stops, then `git rebase --continue`.\\n- On errors — `git rebase --abort`.\\n\\nThe pluses:\\n- A clean, linear history, easy to read the logs.\\n- Makes bisect and the search for errors easier.\\n\\nThe minuses:\\n- It rewrites the history: you must not rebase public branches.\\n\\nThe variants:\\n\\n`git rebase -i` for interactive editing (composing, squashing and reordering commits).",null],[2954,"`git merge` combines two branches: if the changes do not intersect, it creates a fast-forward (it moves the branch\'s pointer). Where there is a divergence, it creates a new \\"merge commit\\", combining the parent branches and preserving both histories. It lets you preserve the information about the merge and the points of divergence.","A fast-forward merge:\\n- If the target branch has no new commits above the base, the pointer is simply moved forward.\\n- The history remains linear, without a merge commit.\\n\\nA three-way merge:\\n- Where the branches diverge, Git finds the common ancestor and then applies the changes of both branches.\\n- A new commit with two parents is created.\\n\\nMerge conflicts:\\n- If one and the same place has been changed in both branches, Git will stop and ask you to resolve the conflict.\\n- Once it has been resolved — `git add` and `git commit`.\\n\\nThe options:\\n- `--no-ff` forces a merge commit always to be created.\\n- `--squash` combines all of the changes into one commit without preserving the history.\\n\\nConclusion:\\n\\n`merge` lets you integrate parallel development and preserve the context of the branching.",null],[2955,"`git merge` preserves the history of the parallel branches, creating a merge commit where they diverge, while `git rebase` rewrites the history: it carries one branch\'s commits on top of another, creating a linear history without merge commits. Merge shows the actual branchings; rebase simplifies the log but changes the commits\' SHAs.","`git merge`:\\n- Fast-forward: if the target branch has not changed, it simply moves the pointer.\\n- Three-way merge: where two branches diverge, it creates a new commit with two parents.\\n- It preserves the topology of the branchings and the merge commits.\\n\\n`git rebase`:\\n- Finds the common ancestor and \\"detaches\\" the current branch\'s commits.\\n- Applies them one at a time on the updated base, creating new SHAs.\\n- Simplifies the history, removes the superfluous merge commits.\\n\\nThe pluses and the minuses:\\n- Merge: safe, does not change the existing history, convenient for public branches.\\n- Rebase: a linear history, easier to read, but must not be done on shared branches (it rewrites the history).\\n\\nWhen to apply which:\\n- Merge: when combining a finished feature branch into main, in order to preserve the context.\\n- Rebase: when synchronizing a feature branch with main before a merge, or in order to tidy the history up.",null],[2956,"There are two main ways:\\n- `git cherry-pick` — copies a particular commit into the current branch.\\n- An interactive rebase (`git rebase -i`) — lets you reorder or carry across several commits.\\n\\nAn example by way of `cherry-pick`:","- Using `git cherry-pick`\\n\\nThis method suits the case where one or several particular commits have to be carried across.\\n\\nThe steps:\\n- Find out the hash of the commit that has to be carried across:\\n\\nAn example of the output:\\n\\n- Switch to the branch into which you are carrying the commit:\\n\\n- Perform the `cherry-pick`:\\n\\nWhat will happen:\\n- Git will apply the changes from the commit `abc123` in `target-branch` as a new commit (with a different hash).\\n\\nIf conflicts have arisen:\\n- Resolve them by hand, then perform:\\n\\n- An interactive rebase (`git rebase -i`)\\n\\nSuits the carrying across of several commits or the changing of their order.\\n\\nThe steps:\\n- Switch to the source branch:\\n\\n- Start an interactive rebase:\\n\\nAn editor will open with a list of the commits.\\n- Leave only the commits you need (comment the rest out, putting a `#` at the start of the line).\\n- Save the file and close the editor.\\n- Switch to the target branch and perform the merge:\\n\\n- An alternative: `git merge` with a selection of commits\\n\\nIf every commit from one branch has to be carried into another:\\n\\nImportant particulars\\n\\n- `cherry-pick` creates a new commit (even if the changes are identical).\\n- Rebase rewrites the history — do not use it for branches that have been published.\\n- If a commit depends on other changes, conflicts are possible.\\n\\nAn example of a conflict during a `cherry-pick`: if the file `app.js` has been changed in the commit `abc123`, but that file has already been changed in `target-branch`, Git will ask for the conflict to be resolved by hand.","git log source-branch --oneline\\n\\nabc123 Added a new feature\\ndef456 Fixed a bug\\n\\ngit checkout target-branch\\n\\ngit cherry-pick abc123\\n\\ngit add .\\ngit cherry-pick --continue\\n\\ngit checkout source-branch\\n\\ngit rebase -i target-branch\\n\\ngit checkout target-branch\\ngit merge source-branch\\n\\ngit checkout target-branch\\ngit merge source-branch"],[2957,"`git pull` is a command that does two things at once: it downloads the changes from the remote server (`git fetch`) and then tries to combine them with your current branch automatically (`git merge`). `git merge` is only the second part of that process; it combines the changes from another branch (from the one you have just downloaded, for example) into your current one.","The `git pull` and `git merge` commands serve to combine changes from different sources, but they do so at different levels of automation.\\n\\n`git pull`\\n- It is a composite command, which performs two operations one after another:\\n- `git fetch`: downloads all of the latest changes from the remote repository (from GitHub, for example) into your local copy. Those changes do not touch your working branch yet.\\n- `git merge`: once the changes have been downloaded, `git pull` tries automatically to combine (`merge`) the corresponding remote branch (most often `origin/your-branch-name`) with your current branch.\\n\\n`git merge`\\n- It is a self-contained, lower-level command. It is used to merge any branch with your current branch. That can be:\\n- The merging of another local branch.\\n- The merging of a remote branch that you have downloaded beforehand by means of `git fetch`.\\n\\nThe key difference:\\n- `git pull = git fetch + git merge`\\n- `git merge` is only one of the two operations that `git pull` performs.\\n\\nWhen to use which:\\n- Use `git pull` in order to synchronize your local branch with the remote repository quickly.\\n- Use `git fetch` and then `git merge` separately if you want to check the changes you have downloaded before combining them with your own work. That is considered the safer approach.",null],[2958,"You cannot simply agree to an unrealistic deadline. You have to hold a dialogue with the PM: explain what the two-week estimate is made up of, and ask which part of the functionality is the least critical. Then find a solution together: simplify the task, move part of the functionality to the next sprint, or enlarge the team temporarily. The main thing is to make the risks of failure visible and manageable.","The situation calls for a professional approach based on transparency and solving the problem together.\\n\\nThe plan of action:\\n- Preparation and argument\\n- Prepare a clear decomposition of the task. Show the PM what subtasks the work is broken into and how much time each one requires.\\n- Explain the technical risks and dependencies that have been taken into account in the estimate.\\n\\n- Dialogue and the search for a compromise\\n- Ask about the priorities: \\"Which part of the functionality can we simplify or postpone, so as to fit into the week but still give the user value?\\"\\n- Propose the options:\\n- Release an MVP (Minimum Viable Product) with the core functionality in a week, and finish it in the next release.\\n- \\"Trim\\" the feature: remove the optional animations, simplify the UI, drop support for the edge cases.\\n- Bring another developer in to help temporarily.\\n\\n- Managing the expectations\\n- Convey the consequences clearly: \\"If we try to do it all in a week, we will either not manage it or release an unstable product with bugs that we will then spend a long time fixing.\\"\\n- Propose an intermediate check-in after a week, in order to show the progress and adjust the plan if that is necessary.\\n\\nWhat you should not do:\\n- Agree silently and then work through the nights (that leads to burnout and low quality).\\n- Insist on your own way straight away, without proposing alternatives.\\n\\nConclusion: your role is not merely to carry tasks out but to give an expert technical assessment. An open dialogue with the PM about the realities and the constraints helps you find the optimal solution for the business and for the project, rather than merely carrying out an unrealistic plan.",null],[2959,"When there are no resources and the task is yours, you have to act along three lines: 1) Reconsider the task — can it be radically simplified or automated? 2) Reconsider the process — eliminate every \\"time eater\\" (meetings, context switches). 3) Reconsider the priorities — agree to postpone or cancel your other, less important tasks, in order to free time up for the critical one.","Solving a complex task on your own requires a systematic approach and a focus on effectiveness.\\n\\nThe strategy for solving it:\\n- Analysis and simplification (the most important)\\n- Decomposition: break the task into the smallest subtasks you can.\\n- The search for a \\"magic button\\": is there a ready-made solution, library or tool that can do 80 % of the work?\\n- Simplifying the goal: what is the absolute minimum for \\"solving\\" the task? Can a simplified version be released now and improved later?\\n\\n- Optimizing the working process\\n- Protecting the focus: block time in the calendar for deep work. Switch the notifications off.\\n- Automation: write scripts for the routine operations (builds, deploys, code generation).\\n- The \\"two-minute rule\\": if a subtask can be solved in two minutes, do it straight away.\\n\\n- Communication and the reconsideration of priorities\\n- Report your load to the management clearly: \\"I can take this task on, but for that I will have to stop the work on X and Y. Are you sure that is the right priority?\\"\\n- That is not a refusal but a request for help in setting the priorities.\\n\\n- Technical tactics\\n- Prototyping: first make a \\"dirty\\" working prototype, in order to test the idea, and then refactor.\\n- Deferred perfectionism: first implement the functionality, then deal with the optimization and the cleanliness of the code, if there is time left.\\n\\nConclusion: the key to solving it is not in working more but in working smarter. A focus on minimizing the volume of work, eliminating the waste of time and communicating clearly about the priorities often lets you find a way out where it seems there is none.",null],[2960,"`git reset` moves the current branch\'s pointer to a particular commit, potentially removing the newer commits from the history, and can change the index and the working directory. `git revert` creates a new commit that undoes the changes of the commit specified, while preserving the whole history of the project. Reset changes the history and is dangerous for commits that have been published, whereas revert is safe for the shared history and adds a new record.","Reset and revert are two fundamentally different Git commands for managing the history of changes.\\n\\ngit reset — rewriting the history\\nReset moves the current branch\'s pointer to the commit specified.\\n\\nThe three modes of reset\\n\\n1. --soft — only moves the pointer\\nbash\\n\\n- The history — removes the last commit\\n- The index — the changes stay in the staged state\\n- The working directory — the files are not changed\\n\\n2. --mixed (the default) — moves the pointer and resets the index\\nbash\\n\\n- The history — removes the last commit\\n- The index — the changes become unstaged\\n- The working directory — the files keep the changes\\n\\n3. --hard — the complete removal of the changes\\nbash\\n\\n- The history — removes the last commit\\n- The index — is cleared completely\\n- The working directory — every change is removed\\n\\ngit revert — the safe undoing of changes\\nRevert creates a new commit that undoes the changes of the commit specified.\\n\\nbash\\n\\nThe characteristics of revert\\n\\n- The history — adds a new commit\\n- Safety — does not rewrite the existing history\\n- Collaboration — safe for commits that have been published\\n\\nA comparison in practice\\n\\nA scenario for using reset\\nbash\\n\\nA scenario for using revert\\nbash\\n\\nThe key differences\\n\\nSafety\\n\\n- Reset — dangerous for a history that has been published\\n- Revert — safe for shared work\\n\\nThe effect on the history\\n\\n- Reset — removes commits from the history\\n- Revert — adds new commits to the history\\n\\nUse in a team\\n\\n- Reset — only for local, unpublished changes\\n- Revert — ideal for fixing errors in a shared branch\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- For local work — use reset in order to rewrite the history\\n- For a fix in master/main — always use revert\\n- For clearing unpublished changes up — reset --hard\\n- For undoing a particular commit — revert with the hash specified\\n\\nConclusion: choose reset for the local reworking of an unpublished history and revert for the safe fixing of errors in commits that have been published. Reset changes the history; revert adds to it.","git reset --soft HEAD~1\\n\\ngit reset --mixed HEAD~1\\n\\ngit reset --hard HEAD~1\\n\\ngit revert HEAD\\n\\n# Local work - we have made several commits, we want to redo them\\ngit add .\\ngit commit -m \\"WIP: incomplete feature\\"\\ngit commit -m \\"More work\\"\\n\\n# We have decided to combine the commits\\ngit reset --soft HEAD~2\\ngit commit -m \\"Complete feature implementation\\"\\n\\n# We have published a commit with an error in master\\ngit commit -m \\"Add feature X\\"\\ngit push origin master\\n\\n# We have discovered the error - we undo it safely\\ngit revert HEAD\\ngit push origin master"],[2961,"In teamwork the Agile approaches are used, which include processes such as Scrum, Kanban, XP and Lean. Agile helps teams work iteratively, adapt to changes and deliver value regularly. The main Agile patterns are the daily stand-up, sprint planning, the retrospective and backlog grooming. Code review, Git flow, CI/CD and the practice of small incremental tasks are also actively applied. The Agile philosophy is aimed at flexibility, transparency and fast feedback.","Team software development is impossible without processes that ensure consistency, predictability and speed. The Agile methodologies are a set of principles and practices that help teams interact effectively and adapt to changes.\\n\\nThe main approaches to teamwork\\n\\n1. Iterative development\\nThe project is broken into small increments, which are delivered regularly.\\n\\n2. The shared ownership of the code\\nEvery developer can work on any part of the project. For that it matters to:\\n- use Git;\\n- organize a quality code review;\\n- synchronize the work through pull requests.\\n\\n3. The automation of the processes\\nThe team uses CI/CD in order to:\\n- run the tests automatically;\\n- build the artifacts;\\n- deliver the product;\\n- reduce the number of errors.\\n\\nThe main Agile methodologies\\n\\nScrum\\nUses fixed iterations (sprints). In Scrum there are:\\n- a Product Owner\\n- a Scrum Master\\n- the development team\\n\\nThe typical ceremonies:\\n- Sprint Planning\\n- The Daily Stand-up\\n- The Sprint Review\\n- The Sprint Retrospective\\n- Backlog Refinement\\n\\nKanban\\nBased on the visualization of the flow of tasks. Uses a board with columns (“To Do → In Progress → Done”). The main value is the management of the flow and the reduction of the WIP (Work In Progress).\\n\\nXP (Extreme Programming)\\nA focus on quality:\\n- pair programming;\\n- TDD;\\n- continuous integration;\\n- refactoring as part of the process.\\n\\nThe key patterns and practices\\n\\n- The backlog — a list of the tasks, requirements and improvements.\\n- User stories — the formalization of the requirements from the user\'s point of view.\\n- The Definition of Done — the criteria for a task\'s readiness.\\n- Velocity — how much the team manages in an iteration.\\n- Planning Poker — the collective estimation of tasks.\\n- The retrospective — the analysis of the process and of the improvements.\\n- The Pull Request Flow — the process of changing the code.\\n- Git flow — the rules of branching.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAgile is not a set of rituals but a philosophy of flexible development. Teams working by Agile deliver value faster, improve their interaction and respond to changes faster, thanks to iterativeness, transparency and the automation of the processes.",null],[2962,"In Agile, tasks normally pass through the stages: Backlog, Ready, In Progress, In Review, In Testing, Done. Each stage denotes a stage of the task\'s life cycle: from the idea to fully finished functionality. Scrum uses similar statuses within a sprint. Kanban relies on the flow: a task moves from right to left until it is finished. These statuses help you manage the work, keep the team\'s load under control and ensure the transparency of the processes.","In Agile it matters to visualize the flow of tasks, so that the team understands what is being carried out, what is ready to be worked on and what is blocked. Different teams use different sets of statuses, but there are several that are generally accepted.\\n\\nThe main statuses of tasks in Agile\\n\\n1. Backlog\\nEvery task, idea and requirement the team has not taken on yet.\\n\\n2. Ready (To Do)\\nThe task is ready to be carried out, has been refined and has been prioritized.\\n\\n3. In Progress\\nA developer is working on the task. At this stage it matters to:\\n- make small commits;\\n- push the changes to a branch;\\n- pass CI regularly.\\n\\n4. In Review\\nThe task is waiting for a code review. At this stage the quality rises and the number of errors in production falls.\\n\\n5. In Testing\\nThe testers check the task or CI runs the automated tests.\\n\\n6. Done\\nThe functionality is ready: it has been tested, deployed and accepted by the user or by the PO.\\n\\nAdditional statuses\\n\\n- Blocked — the task cannot be carried out because of a dependency.\\n- On Hold — the work has been suspended temporarily.\\n- Ready for Release — the task has passed QA and is sitting in the release list.\\n\\nScrum-specific states\\nIn Scrum a task is considered “Done” only if it has passed the Definition of Done:\\n- the tests have been written;\\n- it has passed code review;\\n- the documentation has been done;\\n- it has been deployed to the test stand.\\n\\nKanban-specific states\\nKanban is oriented towards WIP (Work in Progress) limits, so the statuses are normally:\\n\\nConclusion\\nWorking statuses in Agile let you track the movement of tasks transparently, detect the bottlenecks of the process and synchronize the team\'s work. The proper visualization of the statuses is one of the key tools of modern development.","Backlog → Selected → In Progress → Review → Done"],[2963,"In most cases it is safer to use `merge`, especially if the branch is already being used by other developers. `Merge` preserves the history of the commits and does not rewrite it. `Rebase` rewrites the history and can lead to conflicts or to the loss of commits if it is used improperly. `rebase` is therefore better applied only in personal branches or ones that have not been published yet.","When a stand or a feature branch has fallen behind the main branch, it matters to choose a strategy that will not break the history.\\n\\nA definition\\nA definition: merge is the combining of branches with the history preserved. A definition: rebase is the carrying of one branch\'s commits on top of another with the history rewritten.\\n\\nMerge\\nMerge creates an additional merge commit.\\n\\nThe pluses:\\n- safe for shared branches\\n- the history is not rewritten\\n- it is simpler to roll a change back\\n\\nThe minuses:\\n- the history can become less linear\\n\\nRebase\\nRebase “rewrites” the commits as though they had been made later.\\n\\nThe pluses:\\n- a clean, linear history\\n- convenient before a merge into main\\n\\nThe minuses:\\n- dangerous for branches that have already been pushed\\n- can break colleagues\' work\\n\\nA practical recommendation\\n\\n- Shared branches (staging, develop) → `merge`\\n- Personal feature branches → `rebase` before the merge\\n- If there are unfinished tasks and parallel work — safety takes priority\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nWhen stability matters and several people are working on the branch, it is safer to use `merge` and to leave `rebase` for the local preparation of the code.",null],[2964,"During a merge Git combines two branches, creating a merge commit. The commit history of both branches is preserved completely. Git does not change the existing commits. As a result the history becomes branching. It is the safest way of combining branches.","Merge is the standard and the safest way of combining branches in Git.\\n\\nA definition\\nMerge is the operation in which Git combines the changes from one branch into another, preserving the whole commit history.\\n\\nHow merge works\\nWhen a merge is performed:\\n- Git looks for the common base commit\\n- applies the changes of both branches\\n- creates a new merge commit\\n\\nWhat happens to the history\\nThe history:\\n- is not rewritten\\n- preserves every commit\\n- becomes non-linear\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\n`M` is the merge commit.\\n\\nThe merge commit\\nA merge commit:\\n- has two parents\\n- records the fact of the combining\\n- shows which branches were merged\\n\\nThe advantages of merge\\n\\n- it is safe\\n- it does not break the history\\n- it suits shared branches\\n- it requires no force push\\n\\nThe disadvantages\\n\\n- the history becomes more complex\\n- a lot of merge commits appear\\n- the log is harder to read\\n\\nConclusion\\nMerge preserves the complete history of the development and is considered the most reliable way of combining branches, especially in teamwork.","A---B---C---M\\n     \\\\     /\\n      D---E"],[2965,"During a rebase Git rewrites the commit history. The current branch\'s commits are carried onto a new base commit and receive new hashes. As a result the history becomes linear. The old commits are in effect replaced by new ones. That makes the history cleaner but potentially more dangerous.","Rebase is a powerful Git tool, but one that calls for caution.\\n\\nA definition\\nRebase is the operation in which Git carries one branch\'s commits on top of another, rewriting the history.\\n\\nHow rebase works\\nWhen a rebase is performed:\\n- Git finds the common base commit\\n- temporarily \\"removes\\" the current branch\'s commits\\n- applies them on top of the new base\\n- creates new commits with new hashes\\n\\nThe effect on the history\\nAs a result:\\n- the history becomes linear\\n- there are no merge commits\\n- every commit has a new hash\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nBefore:\\n\\nAfter the rebase:\\n\\nWhy this is a rewriting of the history\\nAlthough the changes are the same:\\n- Git creates new commits\\n- the old commits no longer exist in the history\\n\\nThat is precisely the cause of the possible problems.\\n\\nWhen rebase is dangerous\\n\\n- if the branch has already been pushed\\n- if other people are working on it\\n- if a `force push` is required\\n\\nWhen rebase is appropriate\\n\\n- local feature branches\\n- preparing a branch before a merge\\n- simplifying the history\\n\\nConclusion\\nRebase makes the history tidy and linear, but it calls for discipline. It must not be applied to shared branches without a full understanding of the consequences.","A---B---C\\n     \\\\\\n      D---E\\n\\nA---B---C---D\'---E\'"],[2966,"Merge\'s main disadvantage is the complication of the commit history. Merge commits appear, which carry no business logic. The history becomes branching and hard to read. That makes the analysis of the changes more difficult. Merge nonetheless remains the safest way of combining branches.","Merge is a safe but not always convenient tool.\\n\\nThe main disadvantages of merge\\n\\nCluttering the history\\nUnder active development:\\n- a lot of merge commits appear\\n- the history becomes visually complex\\n- `git log` is hard to read\\n\\nNoise in the history\\nA merge commit:\\n- often contains no new changes\\n- merely records the fact of the combining\\n- makes the search for the causes of bugs more complex\\n\\nThe difficulty of analysis\\nWhen analysing it:\\n- it is hard to understand the order of the changes\\n- `git bisect` is harder to do\\n- there is more cognitive load\\n\\nWhy merge is used all the same\\nDespite the disadvantages:\\n- merge does not rewrite the history\\n- it is safe for teamwork\\n- it requires no force push\\n\\nWhere merge is preferable\\n\\n- `main` / `develop`\\n- shared branches\\n- open-source projects\\n\\nConclusion\\nMerge worsens the readability of the history but ensures safety. It is a conscious compromise between the cleanliness of the history and reliability.",null],[2967,"Rebase\'s main risk is the rewriting of the history. The commits receive new hashes, which can break other developers\' work. Using rebase for shared branches requires a force push. That can lead to the loss of commits. Rebase therefore has to be used with caution.","Rebase is dangerous not in itself but when applied improperly.\\n\\nThe main risk — the rewriting of the history\\nDuring a rebase:\\n- the old commits disappear\\n- new commits are created\\n- the references to the old commits break\\n\\nProblems in teamwork\\nIf the branch has already been pushed:\\n- another developer is based on the old history\\n- the rebase changes the base\\n- a conflict of histories arises\\n- manual restoration is required\\n\\nForce push\\nAfter a rebase the following is often required:\\n\\nThat:\\n- can wipe other people\'s commits out\\n- breaks CI\\n- creates chaos\\n\\nThe loss of changes\\nIn the event of a mistake:\\n- the commits can become inaccessible\\n- restoring them requires the `reflog`\\n- it is not always obvious what has been lost\\n\\nWhen there are no risks\\nRebase is safe if:\\n- the branch is local\\n- the branch belongs to a single developer\\n- it has not been published yet\\n\\nConclusion\\nRebase is a tool for careful hands. It is worth using consciously and only where the risk is minimal.","git push --force"],[2968,"GitFlow uses several main branches, each of which is responsible for its own task. The main ones are `main` and `develop`. `feature` branches are used for developing new capabilities. For preparing a release there is `release`, and for urgent fixes `hotfix`. Such a structure helps you put the development and the releases in order.","GitFlow is a branching convention that defines the roles of the various branches and the rules for using them. It is especially useful in team development and with regular releases.\\n\\nThe main branches of GitFlow\\n\\n1. `main`\\nThis branch contains only stable, production-ready code.\\n- `main` always holds the version that can be released to users\\n- Every commit in `main` is normally marked with a version tag\\n- Direct commits to `main` are forbidden\\n\\n2. `develop`\\nThis is the main branch of the active development.\\n- Every finished feature is merged into it\\n- The code may be unstable\\n- `feature`, `release` and sometimes `hotfix` branches are created off it\\n\\nThe supporting branches\\n\\n3. `feature/*`\\nUsed for developing new capabilities.\\n- Created off `develop`\\n- Contain the code of one particular task\\n- Once finished, they are merged back into `develop`\\n\\n4. `release/*`\\nUsed for preparing a release.\\n- Created off `develop`\\n- Contain only bugfixes and version corrections\\n- Once finished, they are merged into `main` and `develop`\\n\\n5. `hotfix/*`\\nUsed for urgent fixes in production.\\n- Created off `main`\\n- Once the fix has been made, they are merged into `main` and `develop`\\n- They let you release patches quickly\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nGitFlow separates stable code, development, features and emergency fixes clearly. It suits projects with a release cycle and several developers well.",null],[2969,"A release in GitFlow begins with the creation of a `release` branch off `develop`. The final fixes and the preparation of the version are carried out in that branch. After that the branch is merged into `main`, where a version tag is created. Then the changes are brought back into `develop`.","The release process in GitFlow is built so as to fix the state of the product and minimize the risks before the release.\\n\\nThe stages of a release\\n\\n1. Preparing for the release\\nWhen the set of features is ready, a release branch is created.\\n\\n- The branch fixes the current state of `develop`\\n- New features are no longer added here\\n\\n2. The final corrections\\nIn the `release` branch the following are carried out:\\n- the fixing of bugs\\n- the updating of the application\'s version\\n- corrections to the configurations and the documentation\\n\\n3. The merge into `main`\\nOnce it has been tested, the release is considered ready.\\n\\n- The code becomes the production version\\n- A version tag is created\\n\\n4. Bringing the changes back into `develop`\\nSo that `develop` does not fall behind production:\\n\\n5. Deleting the release branch\\nAfter the release the temporary branch is no longer needed.\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nA GitFlow release is a controlled process of fixing a version, which lets you stabilize the code before the release and preserve the integrity of the development branches.","git checkout develop\\ngit checkout -b release/1.2.0\\n\\ngit checkout main\\ngit merge release/1.2.0\\ngit tag v1.2.0\\n\\ngit checkout develop\\ngit merge release/1.2.0"],[2970,"The planning of tasks normally happens iteratively, through sprints or kanban. The tasks are formed from the backlog and estimated by the team. The distribution takes the priorities and the developers\' load into account. The frontend takes part in the discussion of the deadlines and the complexity. That helps you make realistic plans.","In most teams the planning and distribution of tasks is built around the Agile processes.\\n\\nWhat the typical planning process looks like\\n\\n- Forming the backlog\\n- the tasks come from the product or from the business\\n- the requirements and the acceptance criteria are described\\n\\n- Prioritization\\n- the business value is determined\\n- the dependencies and the risks are taken into account\\n\\n- Estimating the tasks\\n- story points or hours\\n- a discussion of the complexity of the frontend part\\n- the detection of uncertainties\\n\\n- Distributing the tasks\\n- with the developers\' experience in mind\\n- the current load\\n- the deadlines\\n\\nThe frontend developer\'s role\\nThe frontend:\\n- takes part in the estimation of the tasks\\n- highlights the technical risks\\n- proposes a decomposition\\n- clarifies the API dependencies\\n\\nWhy that matters\\nGood planning:\\n- reduces the number of emergencies\\n- makes the deadlines predictable\\n- improves the quality of the implementation\\n\\nConclusion: effective planning is built on a transparent backlog, joint estimation and the active participation of the frontend developers.",null],[2971,"Code review is needed in order to raise the quality of the code and reduce the number of errors. It helps you find problems before the code reaches production. Code review also levels the style and the approaches within the team. It is a tool for the exchange of knowledge, not for control. A good code review makes the code more intelligible and safer.","Code review is the regular process in which changes are checked by other developers before they are combined into the main branch.\\n\\nThe main tasks of code review\\n\\n- Quality control\\n- the search for logical errors\\n- the detection of potential bugs\\n\\n- Supporting the architecture\\n- conformity to the approaches that have been adopted\\n- preventing an architectural \\"blurring\\"\\n\\n- Improving the readability\\n- intelligible names\\n- the simplification of complex sections\\n\\n- The exchange of knowledge\\n- acquainting the team with new code\\n- the spreading of best practices\\n\\nWhat code review should not do\\n\\n- replace automated tests\\n- turn into subjective criticism\\n- block the work over trifles\\n\\nConclusion\\nCode review is a mechanism of collective responsibility for the codebase and an important element of scalable development.",null],[2972,"Task trackers and planning systems are used for managing tasks. They help you record the requirements, the priorities and the status of the work. Most often the tasks are linked with the repository and with code review. The tools make communication and transparency easier. The choice depends on the size of the team.","Task-management tools are needed in order to coordinate the work and track the progress.\\n\\nThe main categories of tool\\n\\n- Task trackers\\n- the tasks\\n- the statuses\\n- the priorities\\n\\n- Planning tools\\n- boards\\n- sprints\\n- the backlog\\n\\n- Integration with the development\\n- links to a pull request\\n- the automatic updating of the statuses\\n\\nThe role for a frontend developer\\n\\n- an understanding of the current priorities\\n- the tracking of the dependencies\\n- transparency about their own work\\n\\nWhat matters more than the tool\\nNot the tool itself but the agreements within the team and the observance of them.\\n\\nConclusion\\nTask-management tools help you organize the work, but they do not replace communication and responsibility.",null],[2973,"A merge request is used in order to review and discuss changes before they reach the main branch. The developer creates a branch, makes the changes and opens an MR. The code goes through review and automatic checks. Once it has been approved, the changes are merged. That reduces the number of errors in the main branch.","The merge-request process helps you keep the quality of the code under control and reduce the risks in development carried out together.\\n\\nThe general scheme of the process\\nThe typical process looks as follows:\\n- The developer creates a feature branch\\n- Makes the changes and commits the code\\n- Opens a merge request\\n- The checks and the code review take place\\n- The changes are merged into the target branch\\n\\nCode review\\nDuring the review the following is normally checked:\\n- the correctness of the logic\\n- the readability and the style of the code\\n- the conformity to the architectural agreements\\n- the absence of side effects\\n\\nAutomatic checks\\nBefore the merge the following are often run:\\n- linters\\n- tests\\n- the build of the project\\n\\nThat lets you catch errors before they reach the main branch.\\n\\nThe role of a merge request\\nAn MR serves as:\\n- a point for discussing the changes\\n- a mechanism of quality control\\n- a history of the decisions taken\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nA competently built merge-request process raises the stability of frontend code and makes teamwork easier.",null],[2974,"A branching model defines the rules for creating and merging branches. It helps several developers work in parallel. Most often feature branches and main branches for the releases are used. That reduces the number of conflicts and makes quality control easier. The model is chosen to suit the size of the team and the frequency of the releases.","A branching model sets the structure of the work with the repository and affects the stability of the product.\\n\\nA definition\\nA branching model is a set of agreements about which branches exist in the repository and how they are used.\\n\\nThe most widespread approach\\nIn frontend projects the following scheme is met with most often:\\n- `main` or `master` — the stable production version\\n- `develop` — the integration branch\\n- `feature/*` — branches for developing individual tasks\\n- `hotfix/*` — quick fixes for production\\n\\nThe working process\\nThe typical scenario:\\n- the task is taken on\\n- a feature branch is created\\n- the changes go through code review\\n- the branch is merged into the main one\\n\\nWhy that matters\\nA clear branching model:\\n- reduces the number of conflicts\\n- makes the releases easier\\n- makes the history of the changes intelligible\\n- raises the stability of the product\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nA branching model is the foundation of teamwork with Git and one of the key factors in a sustainable development process.",null],[2975,"Code review is the checking of code by other developers before it is combined into the main branch. Normally a developer creates a pull request and their colleagues review the changes. They look for errors, architectural problems and non-conformity to the standards. Once it has been approved, the code is merged into the main branch.","Code review is the process of controlling the quality of code before it reaches the main repository.\\n\\nHow the process goes\\nNormally it looks like this:\\n- The developer creates a branch\\n- Makes the changes\\n- Creates a Pull Request or a Merge Request\\n- The other developers review the code\\n- Corrections are made\\n- The code is accepted and merged\\n\\nWhat is checked at review\\nAttention is normally paid to:\\n- the readability of the code\\n- the architecture\\n- the error handling\\n- the safety\\n- the conformity to the style guide\\n\\nAn example of the comments\\nA review often includes remarks like:\\n\\nWhat code review is for\\nThe main reasons:\\n- a reduction in the number of bugs\\n- the exchange of knowledge within the team\\n- a single style of code\\n- control over the architecture\\n\\nConclusion\\nCode review is an important stage of development, which improves the quality of the code and reduces the likelihood of errors in production.","# better extracted into a separate function\\n# worth adding the handling of the exception"],[2976,"Pre-commit hooks are scripts that are run automatically before a commit is performed. They can check the style of the code and run linters and tests. If a check does not pass, the commit is cancelled. That lets you keep code of poor quality out of the repository.","Pre-commit hooks are automatic checks that are carried out before changes are recorded in Git.\\n\\nHow it works\\nThe process looks like this:\\n- The developer performs a `git commit`\\n- Git runs the hook\\n- The checks are run\\n- If there are errors, the commit is cancelled\\n\\nWhat is normally checked\\nIn practice the following are run most often:\\n- the formatting of the code (black)\\n- linters (flake8)\\n- the sorting of the imports (isort)\\n- the static analysis of the types (mypy)\\n\\nThe pre-commit tool\\nThe most widespread tool is the `pre-commit` library.\\n\\nAn example of the configuration:\\n\\nThe installation:\\n\\nWhat this is for\\nPre-commit:\\n- reduces the number of errors\\n- standardizes the code\\n- automates the checks\\n\\nConclusion\\nPre-commit hooks let you check code automatically before a commit and maintain a uniform quality of code across the team.","repos:\\n  - repo: https://github.com/psf/black\\n    rev: 23.0\\n    hooks:\\n      - id: black\\n\\npre-commit install"],[2977,"The automatic check is normally set up by means of the pre-commit tool. Linters, formatters and other checks are specified in the configuration. Once the hooks have been installed, the checks are run automatically before a commit. That prevents incorrect code from reaching the repository.","The automatic checking of code before a commit is a combination of tools and configuration.\\n\\nThe main steps of the setup\\n\\n- Install pre-commit:\\n\\n- Create the file `.pre-commit-config.yaml`\\n- Add the checks:\\n\\n- Install the hooks:\\n\\nWhat checks are normally added\\nIn practice the following are applied:\\n- black\\n- flake8\\n- isort\\n- mypy\\n\\nAn additional level of checking\\nThe code is often additionally checked in CI:\\n- GitHub Actions\\n- GitLab CI\\n\\nThat guarantees the check will be carried out even if the hooks have been switched off.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn automatic check before a commit reduces the number of errors and makes the code in the repository more stable.","pip install pre-commit\\n\\nrepos:\\n  - repo: https://github.com/psf/black\\n    rev: 23.0\\n    hooks:\\n      - id: black\\n\\npre-commit install"],[2978,"Semantic versioning (SemVer) is used most often, where the version consists of three numbers: major, minor and patch. Major is for incompatible changes, minor for new functionality, patch for error fixes. The versions are normally recorded with tags in Git. That helps you manage the releases and the dependencies.","Versioning is a way of designating the state of a project and the changes between releases.\\n\\nSemantic versioning\\nThe format of a version:\\n\\nThe meanings:\\n- MAJOR — incompatible changes to the API.\\n- MINOR — new functionality without breaking the compatibility.\\n- PATCH — error fixes.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nHow versions are used in practice\\nMost often:\\n- a git tag is created\\n- a release is formed\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nWhere versioning is used\\nVersions matter:\\n- for libraries\\n- for APIs\\n- for deployment\\n\\nConclusion\\nSemantic versioning helps you understand the nature of the changes and manage the releases more predictably.","MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH\\n\\n2.3.1\\n\\ngit tag v1.2.0\\ngit push origin v1.2.0"],[2979,"Commit conventions are the rules for writing commit messages. The Conventional Commits standard, for example, uses prefixes such as feat, fix and docs. Such rules let you generate a changelog automatically and understand the history of the changes. That makes maintaining the project easier.","Commit conventions are a standardized format for commit messages.\\n\\nAn example of Conventional Commits\\nThe format:\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe main types of commit\\nThe following are used most often:\\n- feat — new functionality\\n- fix — a fix\\n- docs — documentation\\n- refactor — refactoring\\n- test — tests\\n\\nWhat this is for\\nCommit conventions let you:\\n- understand the changes quickly\\n- build a changelog automatically\\n- determine the type of the release\\n\\nThe connection with CI/CD\\nSome systems automatically:\\n- increase the version\\n- publish the releases\\n\\nConclusion\\nCommit conventions make the history of the changes structured and let you automate the releases and the documentation.","type(scope): description\\n\\nfeat(auth): add JWT login\\nfix(api): handle empty response"],[2980,"A changelog can be generated automatically if the commit messages are formatted to a standard — Conventional Commits, for example. Special tools analyse the Git history, group the changes and form a list of the innovations and the fixes. semantic-release or conventional-changelog are normally used. That lets you automate the releases and the documentation.","A changelog is a list of the changes between a project\'s versions.\\n\\nHow the generation happens\\nThe process normally looks like this:\\n- The developers write the commit messages to the standard\\n- The tool analyses the Git history\\n- The commits are grouped by type\\n- The changelog is formed\\n\\nAn example of commit messages:\\n\\nThe tool will distribute the changes across the categories:\\n- Features\\n- Bug Fixes\\n- Documentation\\n\\nThe tools\\nIn practice the following are used:\\n- conventional-changelog\\n- semantic-release\\n- git-cliff\\n\\nThey can:\\n- generate a changelog\\n- raise the version\\n- create releases\\n\\nWhere this is used\\nNormally in CI/CD:\\n- when a tag is created\\n- when a release is built\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe automatic generation of a changelog reduces the manual work and makes the history of the changes structured and intelligible.","feat(auth): add login endpoint\\nfix(api): correct validation\\ndocs: update README"],[2981,"A container is a lightweight, isolated runtime environment that uses the host operating system\'s kernel and shared resources. Containers provide isolation at the process level. A virtual machine is a complete isolated system with its own operating system, which runs on virtualised hardware and needs more resources.","Containers: containers run on the same operating system kernel as the host, which lets them be lighter and start quickly. They isolate processes from each other, but all the processes inside a container can use shared system resources such as files, the network and memory.\\nVirtual machines: a virtual machine has a complete environment with its own operating system and resources, independent of the host. That provides full isolation, but it also needs far more resources and time to start.\\nContainers are preferable for fast, lightweight application deployment, while virtual machines are useful for more complex scenarios with full operating systems and high isolation requirements.",null],[2982,"Docker Engine is a client-server platform for creating, running and managing Docker containers. It consists of the Docker daemon (the server) and the command-line interface (CLI), which work together to manage a container\'s life cycle.","Docker Engine comprises two main components:\\n\\n- The Docker daemon (dockerd): this is the server side, which manages containers and their life cycle, and also interacts with other containers and with the host system.\\n\\n- The Docker CLI (docker): this is the client side, which provides the command-line interface for the user. It talks to the Docker daemon through an API and lets you run commands for creating, running and managing containers.\\n\\nDocker Engine manages every aspect of container technology, such as:\\n\\n- Starting and stopping containers\\n\\n- Creating network bridges between containers\\n\\n- Handling volumes for data\\n\\n- Managing containers\' resources\\n\\nDocker Engine is the foundation of the Docker ecosystem, providing container management and interaction with the operating system.",null],[2983,"A Docker image is an executable, lightweight package that includes everything needed to run an application, including the code, libraries and system dependencies. A Docker image is used to create containers.","A Docker image is a ready-to-run package that contains all the files an application needs, such as the source code, libraries, system tools and dependencies.\\n\\n- Docker images are created from a Dockerfile, which holds the instructions for installing every dependency, configuring the environment and starting the application.\\n\\n- When a container is started, Docker uses that image as the template for creating the container. Every container started from the same image will have the same configuration and environment.\\n\\n- Docker images can be downloaded from Docker Hub or other container registries, and can also be built locally from a Dockerfile.\\n\\nA Docker image is the basis for containers in Docker, making it easy to create and run applications in standardised, isolated environments.",null],[2984,"Docker Hub is a cloud service for storing and distributing Docker images. It provides a central repository where developers can find, download and share images, both public and private.","Docker Hub is a service from Docker that serves to store, publish and share Docker images. It lets you keep both public images and private ones available only to particular users.\\n\\n- Through Docker Hub developers can easily get access to images created by other users or by official organisations. That speeds development up, since it lets you use ready-made solutions and templates.\\n\\n- Docker Hub also provides facilities for building images automatically, integrating with CI/CD systems and managing versions.\\n\\nAn example of use: to download an image from Docker Hub you can use the command `docker pull <image_name>`, and to publish your own image, `docker push <image_name>`.\\nDocker Hub is the main tool for finding, storing and distributing Docker images, which makes developing and deploying applications easier.",null],[2985,"To create a Docker container you need to use the `docker run` command, giving the name of the image. For example, `docker run myimage:tag` will create and start a container based on the given image.","To create a container from a Docker image, the `docker run` command is used, followed by the name of the image.\\nThe `docker run` command does not only create the container but starts it immediately as well. You can also add extra options to configure the container, such as ports, volumes and environment variables.\\nAn example command: `docker run -d -p 8080:80 myimage` — it will create a container from the `myimage` image, run it in the background (`-d`) and map the container\'s port 80 to the host\'s port 8080.\\nIf the image is not present locally, Docker will download it from Docker Hub automatically.\\nThe `docker run` command is used to create and start a container from an image, and it also lets you configure how the container runs.",null],[2986,"A Dockerfile is a text file holding the instructions for building a Docker image. It describes the steps for installing dependencies, configuring the environment and running the applications the container needs.","A Dockerfile is a script consisting of a set of commands that describes how a Docker image should be built. Each command in a Dockerfile performs a particular task, for example installing dependencies, copying files or setting environment variables.\\nThe main commands in a Dockerfile include:\\n\\n- `FROM`: states the base image to build on.\\n\\n- `RUN`: executes commands inside the container (installing packages, for example).\\n\\n- `COPY`: copies files from the local system into the container.\\n\\n- `CMD`: states the command that will be executed when the container starts.\\n\\nA Dockerfile lets the image build process be reproduced automatically, which helps maintain consistency across different environments.\\nAn example Dockerfile:\\n\\nWith the `docker build` command an image can be created from this Dockerfile.\\nA Dockerfile is a script that lets you automate the creation of Docker images, providing repeatability and consistency in the build process.","FROM ubuntu:20.04\\nRUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y python3\\nCOPY . /app\\nCMD [\\"python3\\", \\"/app/myapp.py\\"]"],[2987,"A hypervisor is software that lets the host system\'s resources be virtualised, creating several virtual machines (VMs). There are two types of hypervisor: the native (bare-metal) one, which runs directly on the host system, and the hosted hypervisor, which runs through the host\'s operating system.","A hypervisor is software that manages virtual machines (VMs) and distributes the host system\'s resources between them.\\nA hypervisor lets several operating systems run on one physical machine, isolating them from each other effectively. That matters for using hardware resources efficiently and for providing security.\\nThe types of hypervisor:\\n\\n- A native hypervisor (bare-metal hypervisor): it runs directly on the host system and needs no operating system. It interacts with the hardware directly, which improves performance. Example: VMware ESXi.\\n\\n- A hosted hypervisor: it runs on top of the host\'s operating system and uses that system\'s resources. It depends on the host operating system in order to manage the virtual machines. Example: VirtualBox.\\n\\nHypervisors play a key role in server virtualisation and in providing multitasking in data centres.\\nHypervisors are the foundation of virtualisation, letting several operating systems run on a single host. They can be native or hosted, depending on the architecture.",null],[2988,"To build a Docker image you need to use the `docker build` command, giving the path to the directory that contains the Dockerfile. For example, `docker build -t myimage:tag .` will build an image named `myimage` with the tag `tag` from the current directory.","To build a Docker image from a Dockerfile, the `docker build` command is used, which reads the instructions from the Dockerfile and creates the image layer by layer.\\nThe command\'s basic structure: `docker build -t <image_name>:<tag> <path_to_dockerfile>`.\\n\\n- `<image_name>` — the name of the image being built.\\n\\n- `<tag>` — a tag for the image (a version, for example).\\n\\n- `<path_to_dockerfile>` — the path to the directory containing the Dockerfile (a dot points to the current directory).\\n\\nExample:\\n\\nThis example will build an image named `myimage` with the tag `latest`, using the Dockerfile from the current directory.\\nOnce it is built, the image can be used to create containers through the `docker run` command.\\nThe `docker build` command is used to build a Docker image, assembling it from the instructions described in the Dockerfile.","docker build -t myimage:latest ."],[2989,"To start a Docker container, use the command `docker start <container_id>`; to stop a container, use the command `docker stop <container_id>`.","- To start a container, the `docker start` command is used, which takes the container\'s id or its name.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\nThis command will start the container named `mycontainer`.\\n\\n- To stop a container, the `docker stop` command is used, which shuts the container down.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\nThis command will stop the container named `mycontainer`.\\nIt is important to remember that the `docker start` command starts the container in the state in which it was stopped, while `docker stop` shuts it down gracefully.\\nThe `docker start` and `docker stop` commands are used to manage a Docker container\'s state, letting you start and stop containers.","docker start mycontainer\\n\\ndocker stop mycontainer"],[2990,"Docker consists of three main components: the Docker client, the Docker host and the Docker registry. The Docker client interacts with the Docker host by sending commands to manage containers, the Docker host manages the containers and their images, and the Docker registry stores Docker images.","Docker consists of three key components:\\n\\n- The Docker client: this is the interface for interacting with Docker. It sends commands to the Docker host using an API. Commands such as `docker build` and `docker run` are issued through the Docker client.\\n\\n- The Docker host: this is the system that manages Docker containers and images. The Docker host includes:\\n\\n- The Docker daemon: the service that manages containers and their life cycle.\\n\\n- Containers: running instances of Docker images.\\n\\n- Images: the templates for creating containers.\\n\\n- A registry: it stores Docker images.\\n\\n- The Docker registry: this is where Docker images are stored. A registry can be public, Docker Hub for example, or private. Images from a registry can be downloaded and used to create containers.\\n\\nDocker consists of the client, the host and the registry, which together provide the full cycle of working with containers, from creating and managing them to storing images.",null],[2991,"To remove a Docker container, use the `docker rm` command with the container\'s id or name. Example: `docker rm mycontainer` removes the container named `mycontainer`.","The `docker rm` command is used to remove a Docker container. The container must be stopped before it is removed.\\nThe command\'s syntax:\\n\\nFor example, to remove the container named `mycontainer`, use the command:\\n\\nIf the container is currently running, it first has to be stopped with the command `docker stop <container_id_or_name>` and then removed.\\nTo remove several containers you can pass several container ids or names:\\n\\nThe `docker rm` command is used to remove a container. Note that the container must be stopped before it is removed.","docker rm <container_id_or_name>\\n\\ndocker rm mycontainer\\n\\ndocker rm container1 container2 container3"],[2992,"The `docker system prune` command is used to remove all stopped containers and unused networks. It also removes unused images and the cache.","The `docker system prune` command is used to clean the Docker system of all stopped containers, unused networks, dangling images and the cache.\\nThe command\'s syntax:\\n\\nFor a more aggressive clean-up you can use the `-a` option, which removes every unused image (not only the dangling ones):\\n\\nThe command will ask for confirmation before performing the removal. To confirm automatically you can add the `-f` flag:\\n\\nThis is a useful command for keeping the Docker system clean and freeing up space.\\nThe `docker system prune` command clears Docker of unused resources, including stopped containers and networks, which helps keep the system in order.","docker system prune\\n\\ndocker system prune -a\\n\\ndocker system prune -f"],[2993,"As long as the container exists, data is not lost. Data loss can only happen when the container is removed, if the data was not saved in persistent storage.","Docker containers are temporary environments, and data saved inside a container will remain available for as long as the container exists.\\nProblems with data loss can arise if the container is removed, especially if the data was not saved somewhere independent of the container\'s life cycle (in a Docker volume or an external database, for example).\\nTo avoid losing data, it is recommended to use volumes or bind mounts for storing data, since they exist independently of the container\'s state.\\nIf a container has been removed, the data inside it disappears too, unless it was saved in durable storage.\\nConclusion: as long as the container exists, data is not lost. Loss is only possible when the container is removed without the data having been saved in persistent storage.",null],[2994,"Docker Compose is a tool for managing multi-container applications. With a simple YAML file (`docker-compose.yml`) you can configure and start several containers, defining their services, networks and volumes.","Docker Compose is a tool that lets you manage multi-container applications in Docker. It makes it easier to create and run applications made up of several containers.\\nWith a docker-compose.yml file you can describe the application\'s various components, such as:\\n\\n- Services: the individual containers that will be started.\\n\\n- Networks: how the containers communicate with each other.\\n\\n- Volumes: places for storing data.\\n\\nDocker Compose\'s commands let you start, stop and manage the state of every container listed in the `docker-compose.yml` file with a single command, for example:\\n\\nThat makes working with multi-container applications considerably easier, providing simpler configuration and automation.\\nDocker Compose helps you manage multi-container applications, letting you define configurations conveniently through YAML files.","docker-compose up"],[2995,"Docker has no explicit limit on the number of containers. The limits depend on hardware resources, such as the amount of memory and the number of processor cores.","Docker has no fixed limit on the number of containers that can be run. The actual number of containers is limited by the host system\'s resources, though, such as:\\n\\n- RAM: each container consumes part of the memory.\\n\\n- Processor resources: containers can compete for processor resources, which limits the number of containers that can be started.\\n\\nThe number of containers also depends on the complexity of the application, the resources available to a container and how efficiently they use the computing resources.\\nFor example, if an application needs considerable computing power or a large amount of memory, that will limit the number of containers that can be run at the same time.\\nThere is no explicit limit on the number of containers in Docker, but it depends on the hardware resources available.",null],[2996,"Logging at the daemon level concerns Docker\'s own operation (system messages and errors), while container logging focuses on the output generated inside particular containers. The daemon\'s logs hold information about how Docker is working, and a container\'s logs hold data related to the applications running in the containers.","Daemon logging: logging at the daemon level includes several levels of message:\\n1. Debug: every action that happens while the daemon is working.\\n2. Info: information about the course of operation, including errors.\\n3. Error: errors that arose while the daemon was working.\\n4. Fatal: critical errors that bring the daemon to a halt.\\nThe daemon\'s logs help you track Docker\'s own state and errors.\\nContainer logging: for logging at the container level, the command `docker logs <container_id>` is used, which displays the logs generated while the container runs. These logs can include the applications\' output, errors in the applications and other messages that happen inside the container.\\nAn example command for working with container logging:\\n\\nLogging at the container level focuses on the applications and their output, not on Docker itself.\\nDaemon logging relates to Docker\'s own operations, while container logging relates to the applications running inside the containers.","sudo docker logs <container_id>"],[2997,"Docker Compose is used to work with several environments in Docker. You create a separate `docker-compose` file for each environment (`docker-compose.dev.yml`, for example) and start the corresponding environment with the settings it needs from those files.","Docker Compose is a tool that helps you manage multi-container applications using YAML files.\\nTo work with several environments you can create different `docker-compose` files, for example:\\n1. docker-compose.dev.yml for development.\\n2. docker-compose.staging.yml for testing at the staging stage.\\n3. docker-compose.prod.yml for the production environment.\\nIn these files you can configure various parameters, such as:\\n1. The services (containers) that will be started.\\n2. The networks and volumes the application needs in order to work.\\n3. Environment variables and settings for different servers.\\nTo start the application in the environment you need, it is enough to give the corresponding Compose file:\\n\\nFor different environments you can use Docker Compose with a separate configuration for each one, which makes managing and deploying applications easier.","docker-compose -f docker-compose.dev.yml up"],[2998,"Yes, Docker supports IPv6, but only on Linux servers. To enable IPv6 support you need to configure the Docker daemon by changing the `ipv6` setting in the `/etc/docker/daemon.json` configuration file.","Docker and IPv6: Docker supports IPv6, but only on servers running Linux. This support lets IPv6 addressing be used for containers.\\nTo enable IPv6 you need to edit Docker\'s configuration file:\\n\\n- Open the `/etc/docker/daemon.json` file.\\n\\n- Add or change the line:\\n\\n- After that, restart Docker so that the changes take effect.\\n\\n- Enabling IPv6 lets you use a wider range of addresses and improves compatibility with modern networks that support IPv6.\\n\\nDocker supports IPv6, but the feature has to be activated in the Docker daemon\'s configuration on Linux servers.","{\\n    \\"ipv6\\": true\\n}"],[2999,"To scale Docker containers horizontally you can use Docker Swarm or Kubernetes. In Docker Swarm you create a cluster and use the `docker service scale` command in order to scale the number of replicas for a service. For example, the command `docker service scale myservice=5` will increase the number of replicas of the `myservice` service to 5.","Docker Swarm is Docker\'s built-in tool for orchestrating containers. It lets you create a cluster of several Docker nodes (servers) that will work together.\\nFor horizontal scaling you can create a service with the number of replicas you need, using the `docker service scale` command. For example, if you have a service called `myservice`, you can scale it to 5 instances:\\n\\nKubernetes is a more powerful container orchestration tool, which also provides facilities for scaling containers. In Kubernetes this is done with objects of the `ReplicaSet` or `Deployment` type, which scale the number of pods in the cluster automatically.\\nScaling lets you maintain high availability and increase the application\'s performance by distributing the load across several containers.\\nTo scale containers horizontally you can use Docker Swarm or Kubernetes, which let you manage the number of instances of a service and distribute the load.","docker service scale myservice=5"],[3000,"Volumes in Docker are intended for storing and sharing data between containers and the host system. They let data be kept independently of the container\'s life cycle, preserving it when the container is stopped or removed. Volumes can be used for a database, application configurations and logs.","Docker volumes are mechanisms for persisting data, created and managed by Docker, which make data durable independently of a container\'s life cycle.\\nVolumes let you:\\n\\n- Keep data between containers: for example, if containers have to exchange data, volumes provide durable storage for it.\\n\\n- Persist data: data saved in volumes survives even after the container is removed. That matters especially for applications that need data stored permanently, such as databases.\\n\\n- Use data across hosts: volumes can be used to share data between containers running on different hosts.\\n\\nAn example of creating a volume:\\n\\nAn example of mounting a volume into a container:\\n\\nVolumes can hold various kinds of data, such as database files, application configurations, logs and other important data.\\nVolumes in Docker are used to store and share data between containers and the host system, preserving data when containers are removed.","docker volume create myvolume\\n\\ndocker run -v myvolume:/data myimage"]]');export{e as default};